window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2015-06-1", "source": "2015年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay commenting on the saying “Knowledge is a treasure, but practice is the key to it. ” You can cite one example or two to illustrate your point of view. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The saying reminds us that knowledge, like a hidden treasure, is precious, yet it stays locked until practice\nturns the key. I firmly agree: only when we put what we learn into practice can knowledge reveal its true value.\nA telling example is learning to swim. A boy may memorize every rule about breathing and strokes, but he will\nsink the moment he enters the water if he never trains his body. Only by jumping in and practising again and\nagain can his theoretical knowledge become a real skill. The same is true of medical students: however many\ntextbooks they read, they cannot heal a single patient until they operate under a doctor's guidance.\nTherefore, knowledge and practice are two sides of one coin. Knowledge offers us the treasure, while practice\nhands us the key. As students, we should never be content with what we read on paper; instead, we ought to\napply it boldly, so that our learning is finally turned into ability."}, "translation": {"prompt": "2011年是中国城市化(urbanization)进程中的历史性时刻，其城市人口首次超过农村人口。在未来20年里，预计约有3.5亿农村人口将移居到城市。如此规模的城市发展对城市交通来说既是挑战，也是机遇。中国政府一直提倡\"以人为本\"的发展理念，强调人们以公交而不是私家车出行。它还号召建设\"资源节约和环境友好型\"社会。有了这个明确的目标，中国城市就可以更好地规划其发展，并把大量投资转向安全、清洁和经济型交通系统的发展上。", "sample": "The year 2011 was a historic moment in China's urbanization, when its urban population exceeded the rural population for the first time. Over the next two decades, it is estimated that some 350 million rural people will move into cities. Urban development of such a scale is both a challenge and an opportunity for urban transport. The Chinese government has advocated a people-oriented development concept, emphasizing that people should travel by public transport rather than by private cars. It also calls for the building of a resource-saving and environment-friendly society. With this clear goal, Chinese cities can better plan their development and channel huge investment into the development of safe, clean and economical transport systems."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2015-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Innovation, the elixir(灵丹妙药) of progress, has always cost people their jobs. In the Industrial Revolution hand weavers were  __26__  aside by the mechanical loom. Over the past 30 years the digital revolution has  __27__  many of the mid-skill jobs that supported 20th-century middle-class life. Typists, ticket agents, bank tellers and many production-line jobs have been dispensed with, just as the weavers were.\nFor those who believe that technological progress has made the world a better place, such disruption is a natural part of rising  __28__  . Although innovation kills some jobs, it creates new and better ones, as a more  __29__  society becomes richer and its wealthier inhabitants demand more goods and services. A hundred years ago one in three American workers was  __30__  on a farm. Today less than 2% of them produce far more food. The millions freed from the land were not rendered  __31__  , but found better-paid work as the economy grew more sophisticated. Today the pool of secretaries has  __32__  , but there are ever more computer programmers and web designers.\nOptimism remains the right starting-point, but for workers the dislocating effects of technology may make themselves evident faster than its  __33__  . Even if new jobs and wonderful products emerge, in the short term income gaps will widen, causing huge social dislocation and perhaps even changing politics. Technology's  __34__  will feel like a tornado(旋风), hitting the rich world first, but  __35__  sweeping through poorer countries too. No government is prepared for it.", "matchIntro": "Why the Mona Lisa Stands Out", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Have you ever fallen for a novel and been amazed not to find it on lists of great books? Or walked around a sculpture renowned as a classic, struggling to see what the fuss is about? If so, you've probably pondered the question a psychologist, James Cutting, asked himself: How does a work of art come to be considered great?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The intuitive answer is that some works of art are just great: of intrinsically superior quality. The paintings that win prime spots in galleries, get taught in classes and reproduced in books are the ones that have proved their artistic value over time. If you can't see they're superior, that's your problem. It's an intimidatingly neat explanation. But some social scientists have been asking awkward questions of it, raising the possibility that artistic canons(名作目录) are little more than fossilised historical accidents."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cutting, a professor at Cornell University, wondered if a psychological mechanism known as the \"mere-exposure effect\" played a role in deciding which paintings rise to the top of the cultural league. Cutting designed an experiment to test his hunch(直觉). Over a lecture course he regularly showed undergraduates works of impressionism for two seconds at a time. Some of the paintings were canonical, included in art-history books. Others were lesser known but of comparable quality. These were exposed four times as often. Afterwards, the students preferred them to the canonical works, while a control group of students liked the canonical ones best. Cutting's students had grown to like those paintings more simply because they had seen them more."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cutting believes his experiment offers a clue as to how canons are formed. He points out that the most reproduced works of impressionism today tend to have been bought by five or six wealthy and influential collectors in the late 19th century. The preferences of these men bestowed(给予) prestige on certain works, which made the works more likely to be hung in galleries and printed in collections. The fame passed down the years, gaining momentum from mere exposure as it did so. The more people were exposed to, the more they liked it, and the more they liked it, the more it appeared in books, on posters and in big exhibitions. Meanwhile, academics and critics created sophisticated justifications for its preeminence(卓越). After all, it's not just the masses who tend to rate what they see more often more highly. As contemporary artists like Warhol and Damien Hirst have grasped, critics' praise is deeply entwined(交织) with publicity. \"Scholars\", Cutting argues, \"are no different from the public in the effects of mere exposure.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "The process described by Cutting evokes a principle that the sociologist Duncan Watts calls \"cumulative advantage\": once a thing becomes popular, it will tend to become more popular still. A few years ago, Watts, who is employed by Microsoft to study the dynamics of social networks, had a similar experience to Cutting's in another Paris museum. After queuing to see the \"Mona Lisa\" in its climate-controlled bulletproof box at the Louvre, he came away puzzled: why was it considered so superior to the three other Leonardos in the previous chamber, to which nobody seemed to be paying the slightest attention?"}, {"label": "F", "text": "When Watts looked into the history of \"the greatest painting of all time\", he discovered that, for most of its life, the \"Mona Lisa\" remained in relative obscurity. In the 1850s, Leonardo da Vinci was considered no match for giants of Renaissance art like Titian and Raphael, whose works were worth almost ten times as much as the \"Mona Lisa\". It was only in the 20th century that Leonardo's portrait of his patron's wife rocketed to the number-one spot. What propelled it there wasn't a scholarly re-evaluation, but a theft."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In 1911 a maintenance worker at the Louvre walked out of the museum with the \"Mona Lisa\" hidden under his smock(工作服). Parisians were shocked at the theft of a painting to which, until then, they had paid little attention. When the museum reopened, people queued to see the gap where the \"Mona Lisa\" had once hung in a way they had never done for the painting itself. From then on, the \"Mona Lisa\" came to represent Western culture itself."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Although many have tried, it does seem improbable that the painting's unique status can be attributed entirely to the quality of its brushstrokes. It has been said that the subject's eyes follow the viewer around the room. But as the painting's biographer, Donald Sassoon, dryly notes, \"In reality the effect can be obtained from any portrait.\" Duncan Watts proposes that the \"Mona Lisa\" is merely an extreme example of a general rule. Paintings, poems and pop songs are buoyed(使浮起) or sunk by random events or preferences that turn into waves of influence, passing down the generations."}, {"label": "I", "text": "\"Saying that cultural objects have value,\" Brian Eno once wrote, \"is like saying that telephones have conversations.\" Nearly all the cultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in inherited opinion; our preferences are always, to some extent, someone else's. Visitors to the \"Mona Lisa\" know they are about to visit the greatest work of art ever and come away appropriately impressed—or let down. An audience at a performance of \"Hamlet\" knows it is regarded as a work of genius, so that is what they mostly see. Watts even calls the preeminence of Shakespeare a \"historical accident\"."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Although the rigid high-low distinction fell apart in the 1960s, we still use culture as a badge of identity. Today's fashion for eclecticism(折衷主义)—\"I love Bach, ABBA and Jay Z\"—is, Shamus Khan, a Columbia University psychologist, argues, a new way for the middle class to distinguish themselves from what they perceive to be the narrow tastes of those beneath them in the social hierarchy."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The intrinsic quality of a work of art is starting to seem like its least important attribute. But perhaps it's more significant than our social scientists allow. First of all, a work needs a certain quality to be eligible to be swept to the top of the pile. The \"Mona Lisa\" may not be a worthy world champion, but it was in the Louvre in the first place, and not by accident. Secondly, some stuff is simply better than other stuff. Read \"Hamlet\" after reading even the greatest of Shakespeare's contemporaries, and the difference may strike you as unarguable."}, {"label": "L", "text": "A study in the British Journal of Aesthetics suggests that the exposure effect doesn't work the same way on everything, and points to a different conclusion about how canons are formed. The social scientists are right to say that we should be a little sceptical of greatness, and that we should always look in the next room. Great art and mediocrity(平庸) can get confused, even by experts. But that's why we need to see, and read, as much as we can. The more we're exposed to the good and the bad, the better we are at telling the difference. The eclecticists have it."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "When the right person is holding the right job at the right moment, that person's influence is greatly expanded. That is the position in which Janet Yellen, who is expected to be confirmed as the next chair of the Federal Reserve Bank (Fed) in January, now finds herself. If you believe, as many do, that unemployment is the major economic and social concern of our day, then it is no stretch to think Yellen is the most powerful person in the world right now.\nThroughout the 2008 financial crisis and the recession and recovery that followed, central banks have taken on the role of stimulators of last resort, holding up the global economy with vast amounts of money in the form of asset buying. Yellen, previously a Fed vice chair, was one of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump. A star economist known for her groundbreaking work on labor markets, Yellen was a kind of prophetess early on in the crisis for her warnings about the subprime(次级债) meltdown. Now it will be her job to get the Fed and the markets out of the biggest and most unconventional monetary program in history without derailing the fragile recovery.\nThe good news is that Yellen, 67, is particularly well suited to meet these challenges. She has a keen understanding of financial markets, an appreciation for their imperfections and a strong belief that human suffering was more related to unemployment than anything else.\nSome experts worry that Yellen will be inclined to chase unemployment to the neglect of inflation. But with wages still relatively flat and the economy increasingly divided between the well-off and the long-term unemployed, more people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩) that would aggravate the economy's problems.\nEither way, the incoming Fed chief will have to walk a fine line in slowly ending the stimulus. It must be steady enough to deflate bubbles(去泡沫) and bring markets back down to earth but not so quick that it creates another credit crisis.\nUnlike many past Fed leaders, Yellen is not one to buy into the finance industry's argument that it should be left alone to regulate itself. She knows all along the Fed has been too slack on regulation of finance.\nYellen is likely to address the issue right after she pushes unemployment below 6%, stabilizes markets and makes sure that the recovery is more inclusive and robust. As Princeton Professor Alan Blinder says, \"She's smart as a whip, deeply logical, willing to argue but also a good listener. She can persuade without creating hostility.\" All those traits will be useful as the global economy's new power player takes on its most annoying problems.", "47": "When the right person is holding the right job at the right moment, that person's influence is greatly expanded. That is the position in which Janet Yellen, who is expected to be confirmed as the next chair of the Federal Reserve Bank (Fed) in January, now finds herself. If you believe, as many do, that unemployment is the major economic and social concern of our day, then it is no stretch to think Yellen is the most powerful person in the world right now.\nThroughout the 2008 financial crisis and the recession and recovery that followed, central banks have taken on the role of stimulators of last resort, holding up the global economy with vast amounts of money in the form of asset buying. Yellen, previously a Fed vice chair, was one of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump. A star economist known for her groundbreaking work on labor markets, Yellen was a kind of prophetess early on in the crisis for her warnings about the subprime(次级债) meltdown. Now it will be her job to get the Fed and the markets out of the biggest and most unconventional monetary program in history without derailing the fragile recovery.\nThe good news is that Yellen, 67, is particularly well suited to meet these challenges. She has a keen understanding of financial markets, an appreciation for their imperfections and a strong belief that human suffering was more related to unemployment than anything else.\nSome experts worry that Yellen will be inclined to chase unemployment to the neglect of inflation. But with wages still relatively flat and the economy increasingly divided between the well-off and the long-term unemployed, more people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩) that would aggravate the economy's problems.\nEither way, the incoming Fed chief will have to walk a fine line in slowly ending the stimulus. It must be steady enough to deflate bubbles(去泡沫) and bring markets back down to earth but not so quick that it creates another credit crisis.\nUnlike many past Fed leaders, Yellen is not one to buy into the finance industry's argument that it should be left alone to regulate itself. She knows all along the Fed has been too slack on regulation of finance.\nYellen is likely to address the issue right after she pushes unemployment below 6%, stabilizes markets and makes sure that the recovery is more inclusive and robust. As Princeton Professor Alan Blinder says, \"She's smart as a whip, deeply logical, willing to argue but also a good listener. She can persuade without creating hostility.\" All those traits will be useful as the global economy's new power player takes on its most annoying problems.", "48": "When the right person is holding the right job at the right moment, that person's influence is greatly expanded. That is the position in which Janet Yellen, who is expected to be confirmed as the next chair of the Federal Reserve Bank (Fed) in January, now finds herself. If you believe, as many do, that unemployment is the major economic and social concern of our day, then it is no stretch to think Yellen is the most powerful person in the world right now.\nThroughout the 2008 financial crisis and the recession and recovery that followed, central banks have taken on the role of stimulators of last resort, holding up the global economy with vast amounts of money in the form of asset buying. Yellen, previously a Fed vice chair, was one of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump. A star economist known for her groundbreaking work on labor markets, Yellen was a kind of prophetess early on in the crisis for her warnings about the subprime(次级债) meltdown. Now it will be her job to get the Fed and the markets out of the biggest and most unconventional monetary program in history without derailing the fragile recovery.\nThe good news is that Yellen, 67, is particularly well suited to meet these challenges. She has a keen understanding of financial markets, an appreciation for their imperfections and a strong belief that human suffering was more related to unemployment than anything else.\nSome experts worry that Yellen will be inclined to chase unemployment to the neglect of inflation. But with wages still relatively flat and the economy increasingly divided between the well-off and the long-term unemployed, more people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩) that would aggravate the economy's problems.\nEither way, the incoming Fed chief will have to walk a fine line in slowly ending the stimulus. It must be steady enough to deflate bubbles(去泡沫) and bring markets back down to earth but not so quick that it creates another credit crisis.\nUnlike many past Fed leaders, Yellen is not one to buy into the finance industry's argument that it should be left alone to regulate itself. She knows all along the Fed has been too slack on regulation of finance.\nYellen is likely to address the issue right after she pushes unemployment below 6%, stabilizes markets and makes sure that the recovery is more inclusive and robust. As Princeton Professor Alan Blinder says, \"She's smart as a whip, deeply logical, willing to argue but also a good listener. She can persuade without creating hostility.\" All those traits will be useful as the global economy's new power player takes on its most annoying problems.", "49": "When the right person is holding the right job at the right moment, that person's influence is greatly expanded. That is the position in which Janet Yellen, who is expected to be confirmed as the next chair of the Federal Reserve Bank (Fed) in January, now finds herself. If you believe, as many do, that unemployment is the major economic and social concern of our day, then it is no stretch to think Yellen is the most powerful person in the world right now.\nThroughout the 2008 financial crisis and the recession and recovery that followed, central banks have taken on the role of stimulators of last resort, holding up the global economy with vast amounts of money in the form of asset buying. Yellen, previously a Fed vice chair, was one of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump. A star economist known for her groundbreaking work on labor markets, Yellen was a kind of prophetess early on in the crisis for her warnings about the subprime(次级债) meltdown. Now it will be her job to get the Fed and the markets out of the biggest and most unconventional monetary program in history without derailing the fragile recovery.\nThe good news is that Yellen, 67, is particularly well suited to meet these challenges. She has a keen understanding of financial markets, an appreciation for their imperfections and a strong belief that human suffering was more related to unemployment than anything else.\nSome experts worry that Yellen will be inclined to chase unemployment to the neglect of inflation. But with wages still relatively flat and the economy increasingly divided between the well-off and the long-term unemployed, more people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩) that would aggravate the economy's problems.\nEither way, the incoming Fed chief will have to walk a fine line in slowly ending the stimulus. It must be steady enough to deflate bubbles(去泡沫) and bring markets back down to earth but not so quick that it creates another credit crisis.\nUnlike many past Fed leaders, Yellen is not one to buy into the finance industry's argument that it should be left alone to regulate itself. She knows all along the Fed has been too slack on regulation of finance.\nYellen is likely to address the issue right after she pushes unemployment below 6%, stabilizes markets and makes sure that the recovery is more inclusive and robust. As Princeton Professor Alan Blinder says, \"She's smart as a whip, deeply logical, willing to argue but also a good listener. She can persuade without creating hostility.\" All those traits will be useful as the global economy's new power player takes on its most annoying problems.", "50": "When the right person is holding the right job at the right moment, that person's influence is greatly expanded. That is the position in which Janet Yellen, who is expected to be confirmed as the next chair of the Federal Reserve Bank (Fed) in January, now finds herself. If you believe, as many do, that unemployment is the major economic and social concern of our day, then it is no stretch to think Yellen is the most powerful person in the world right now.\nThroughout the 2008 financial crisis and the recession and recovery that followed, central banks have taken on the role of stimulators of last resort, holding up the global economy with vast amounts of money in the form of asset buying. Yellen, previously a Fed vice chair, was one of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump. A star economist known for her groundbreaking work on labor markets, Yellen was a kind of prophetess early on in the crisis for her warnings about the subprime(次级债) meltdown. Now it will be her job to get the Fed and the markets out of the biggest and most unconventional monetary program in history without derailing the fragile recovery.\nThe good news is that Yellen, 67, is particularly well suited to meet these challenges. She has a keen understanding of financial markets, an appreciation for their imperfections and a strong belief that human suffering was more related to unemployment than anything else.\nSome experts worry that Yellen will be inclined to chase unemployment to the neglect of inflation. But with wages still relatively flat and the economy increasingly divided between the well-off and the long-term unemployed, more people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩) that would aggravate the economy's problems.\nEither way, the incoming Fed chief will have to walk a fine line in slowly ending the stimulus. It must be steady enough to deflate bubbles(去泡沫) and bring markets back down to earth but not so quick that it creates another credit crisis.\nUnlike many past Fed leaders, Yellen is not one to buy into the finance industry's argument that it should be left alone to regulate itself. She knows all along the Fed has been too slack on regulation of finance.\nYellen is likely to address the issue right after she pushes unemployment below 6%, stabilizes markets and makes sure that the recovery is more inclusive and robust. As Princeton Professor Alan Blinder says, \"She's smart as a whip, deeply logical, willing to argue but also a good listener. She can persuade without creating hostility.\" All those traits will be useful as the global economy's new power player takes on its most annoying problems.", "51": "Air pollution is deteriorating in many places around the world. The fact that public parks in cities become crowded as soon as the sun shines proves that people long to breathe in green, open spaces. They do not all know what they are seeking but they flock there, nevertheless. And, in these surroundings, they are generally both peaceful and peaceable. It is rare to see people fighting in a garden. Perhaps struggle unfolds first, not at an economic or social level, but over the appropriation of air, essential to life itself. If human beings can breathe and share air, they don't need to struggle with one another.\nUnfortunately, in our western tradition, neither materialist nor idealist theoreticians give enough consideration to this basic condition for life. As for politicians, despite proposing curbs on environmental pollution, they have not yet called for it to be made a crime. Wealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay for it.\nBut is our life worth anything other than money? The plant world shows us in silence what faithfulness to life consists of. It also helps us to a new beginning, urging us to care for our breath, not only at a vital but also at a spiritual level. The interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention is that which exists between ourselves and the plant world. Often described as \"the lungs of the planet\", the woods that cover the earth offer us the gift of breathable air by releasing oxygen. But their capacity to renew the air polluted by industry has long reached its limit. If we lack the air necessary for a healthy life, it is because we have filled it with chemicals and undercut the ability of plants to regenerate it. As we know, rapid deforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels is an explosive recipe for an irreversible disaster.\nThe fight over the appropriation of resources will lead the entire planet to hell unless humans learn to share life, both with each other and with plants. This task is simultaneously ethical and political because it can be discharged only when each takes it upon herself or himself and only when it is accomplished together with others. The lesson taught by plants is that sharing life expands and enhances the sphere of the living, while dividing life into so-called natural or human resources diminishes it. We must come to view the air, the plants and ourselves as the contributors to the preservation of life and growth, rather than a web of quantifiable objects or productive potentialities at our disposal. Perhaps then we would finally begin to live, rather than being concerned with bare survival.", "52": "Air pollution is deteriorating in many places around the world. The fact that public parks in cities become crowded as soon as the sun shines proves that people long to breathe in green, open spaces. They do not all know what they are seeking but they flock there, nevertheless. And, in these surroundings, they are generally both peaceful and peaceable. It is rare to see people fighting in a garden. Perhaps struggle unfolds first, not at an economic or social level, but over the appropriation of air, essential to life itself. If human beings can breathe and share air, they don't need to struggle with one another.\nUnfortunately, in our western tradition, neither materialist nor idealist theoreticians give enough consideration to this basic condition for life. As for politicians, despite proposing curbs on environmental pollution, they have not yet called for it to be made a crime. Wealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay for it.\nBut is our life worth anything other than money? The plant world shows us in silence what faithfulness to life consists of. It also helps us to a new beginning, urging us to care for our breath, not only at a vital but also at a spiritual level. The interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention is that which exists between ourselves and the plant world. Often described as \"the lungs of the planet\", the woods that cover the earth offer us the gift of breathable air by releasing oxygen. But their capacity to renew the air polluted by industry has long reached its limit. If we lack the air necessary for a healthy life, it is because we have filled it with chemicals and undercut the ability of plants to regenerate it. As we know, rapid deforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels is an explosive recipe for an irreversible disaster.\nThe fight over the appropriation of resources will lead the entire planet to hell unless humans learn to share life, both with each other and with plants. This task is simultaneously ethical and political because it can be discharged only when each takes it upon herself or himself and only when it is accomplished together with others. The lesson taught by plants is that sharing life expands and enhances the sphere of the living, while dividing life into so-called natural or human resources diminishes it. We must come to view the air, the plants and ourselves as the contributors to the preservation of life and growth, rather than a web of quantifiable objects or productive potentialities at our disposal. Perhaps then we would finally begin to live, rather than being concerned with bare survival.", "53": "Air pollution is deteriorating in many places around the world. The fact that public parks in cities become crowded as soon as the sun shines proves that people long to breathe in green, open spaces. They do not all know what they are seeking but they flock there, nevertheless. And, in these surroundings, they are generally both peaceful and peaceable. It is rare to see people fighting in a garden. Perhaps struggle unfolds first, not at an economic or social level, but over the appropriation of air, essential to life itself. If human beings can breathe and share air, they don't need to struggle with one another.\nUnfortunately, in our western tradition, neither materialist nor idealist theoreticians give enough consideration to this basic condition for life. As for politicians, despite proposing curbs on environmental pollution, they have not yet called for it to be made a crime. Wealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay for it.\nBut is our life worth anything other than money? The plant world shows us in silence what faithfulness to life consists of. It also helps us to a new beginning, urging us to care for our breath, not only at a vital but also at a spiritual level. The interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention is that which exists between ourselves and the plant world. Often described as \"the lungs of the planet\", the woods that cover the earth offer us the gift of breathable air by releasing oxygen. But their capacity to renew the air polluted by industry has long reached its limit. If we lack the air necessary for a healthy life, it is because we have filled it with chemicals and undercut the ability of plants to regenerate it. As we know, rapid deforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels is an explosive recipe for an irreversible disaster.\nThe fight over the appropriation of resources will lead the entire planet to hell unless humans learn to share life, both with each other and with plants. This task is simultaneously ethical and political because it can be discharged only when each takes it upon herself or himself and only when it is accomplished together with others. The lesson taught by plants is that sharing life expands and enhances the sphere of the living, while dividing life into so-called natural or human resources diminishes it. We must come to view the air, the plants and ourselves as the contributors to the preservation of life and growth, rather than a web of quantifiable objects or productive potentialities at our disposal. Perhaps then we would finally begin to live, rather than being concerned with bare survival.", "54": "Air pollution is deteriorating in many places around the world. The fact that public parks in cities become crowded as soon as the sun shines proves that people long to breathe in green, open spaces. They do not all know what they are seeking but they flock there, nevertheless. And, in these surroundings, they are generally both peaceful and peaceable. It is rare to see people fighting in a garden. Perhaps struggle unfolds first, not at an economic or social level, but over the appropriation of air, essential to life itself. If human beings can breathe and share air, they don't need to struggle with one another.\nUnfortunately, in our western tradition, neither materialist nor idealist theoreticians give enough consideration to this basic condition for life. As for politicians, despite proposing curbs on environmental pollution, they have not yet called for it to be made a crime. Wealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay for it.\nBut is our life worth anything other than money? The plant world shows us in silence what faithfulness to life consists of. It also helps us to a new beginning, urging us to care for our breath, not only at a vital but also at a spiritual level. The interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention is that which exists between ourselves and the plant world. Often described as \"the lungs of the planet\", the woods that cover the earth offer us the gift of breathable air by releasing oxygen. But their capacity to renew the air polluted by industry has long reached its limit. If we lack the air necessary for a healthy life, it is because we have filled it with chemicals and undercut the ability of plants to regenerate it. As we know, rapid deforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels is an explosive recipe for an irreversible disaster.\nThe fight over the appropriation of resources will lead the entire planet to hell unless humans learn to share life, both with each other and with plants. This task is simultaneously ethical and political because it can be discharged only when each takes it upon herself or himself and only when it is accomplished together with others. The lesson taught by plants is that sharing life expands and enhances the sphere of the living, while dividing life into so-called natural or human resources diminishes it. We must come to view the air, the plants and ourselves as the contributors to the preservation of life and growth, rather than a web of quantifiable objects or productive potentialities at our disposal. Perhaps then we would finally begin to live, rather than being concerned with bare survival.", "55": "Air pollution is deteriorating in many places around the world. The fact that public parks in cities become crowded as soon as the sun shines proves that people long to breathe in green, open spaces. They do not all know what they are seeking but they flock there, nevertheless. And, in these surroundings, they are generally both peaceful and peaceable. It is rare to see people fighting in a garden. Perhaps struggle unfolds first, not at an economic or social level, but over the appropriation of air, essential to life itself. If human beings can breathe and share air, they don't need to struggle with one another.\nUnfortunately, in our western tradition, neither materialist nor idealist theoreticians give enough consideration to this basic condition for life. As for politicians, despite proposing curbs on environmental pollution, they have not yet called for it to be made a crime. Wealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay for it.\nBut is our life worth anything other than money? The plant world shows us in silence what faithfulness to life consists of. It also helps us to a new beginning, urging us to care for our breath, not only at a vital but also at a spiritual level. The interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention is that which exists between ourselves and the plant world. Often described as \"the lungs of the planet\", the woods that cover the earth offer us the gift of breathable air by releasing oxygen. But their capacity to renew the air polluted by industry has long reached its limit. If we lack the air necessary for a healthy life, it is because we have filled it with chemicals and undercut the ability of plants to regenerate it. As we know, rapid deforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels is an explosive recipe for an irreversible disaster.\nThe fight over the appropriation of resources will lead the entire planet to hell unless humans learn to share life, both with each other and with plants. This task is simultaneously ethical and political because it can be discharged only when each takes it upon herself or himself and only when it is accomplished together with others. The lesson taught by plants is that sharing life expands and enhances the sphere of the living, while dividing life into so-called natural or human resources diminishes it. We must come to view the air, the plants and ourselves as the contributors to the preservation of life and growth, rather than a web of quantifiable objects or productive potentialities at our disposal. Perhaps then we would finally begin to live, rather than being concerned with bare survival."}, "questions": [{"id": "2015-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the woman intend to do?", "options": ["A) Persuade the man to join her company.", "B) Employ the most up-to-date technology.", "C) Export bikes to foreign markets.", "D) Expand their domestic business."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Export bikes to foreign markets.\n【定位】I agree. That's why I'm suggesting exporting, because I feel we can compete with the best\nof them.\n我同意。这正是我提议出口的原因，因为我觉得我们能跟最好的厂商一较高下。\n【信号】本人 I'm suggesting 直接表态。女方用 I'm suggesting exporting 第一人称直陈自己的\n主张，这种说话人本人 I'm suggesting / I want 的直接表态句，就是意图题的答案信号。\n【替换】I'm suggesting exporting ↔ Export bikes to foreign markets\nexporting=出口到海外市场\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音开头女方说 get an up-to-date picture of where we stand now，是要先摸清现状这\n个最新的全貌；选项把 up-to-date 偷接到 technology 上变成采用最新技术，纯属盗词错\n配，女方从没说要上新技术。\n借用原文：get an up-to-date picture of where we stand now\nD) 张冠李戴\nExpand their domestic business 对应的是男方那句 Why don't we just concentrate on\nexpanding here at home?，是男方的提议、也是女方要劝离的方向；女方虽承认要 hold\non to our position here，但她的意图是往外出口，不是扩张国内。\n借用原文：Why don't we just concentrate on expanding here at home?\nA) 未提及\n整段对话女方没有任何劝男方加入她公司的内容，Persuade the man to join her\ncompany 是凭空添加的情节，录音里两人本就是同事在商量公司决策。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why does the man think it's safe to focus on the home market?", "options": ["A) The state subsidizes small and medium enterprises.", "B) The government has control over bicycle imports.", "C) They can compete with the best domestic manufacturers.", "D) They have a cost advantage and can charge higher prices."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The government has control over bicycle imports.\n【定位】Yes, but it's safe. The government keeps out foreigners with import controls, though I must\nadmit I feel sure we could hold our own against foreign bikes.\n是的，但这样安全。政府用进口管制把外国人挡在外面，不过我得承认，我确信我们也能跟\n外国自行车一较高下。\n【信号】safe 后紧跟的因果解释。男方先说 it's safe，紧接着 The government keeps out\nforeigners with import controls 就是对 safe 的因果解释，安全的原因正落在这句对 safe\n的说明上。\n【替换】The government keeps out foreigners with import controls ↔\nThe government has control over bicycle imports\n挡外人=管控进口\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 compete with the best 一类说法（女方 we can compete with the best of\nthem、男方 hold our own against foreign bikes），但那是讲出口也有竞争力，不是守\n国内安全的原因；选项 They can compete with the best domestic manufacturers 还把\n对象偷换成 domestic 国内厂商。\n借用原文：I feel sure we could hold our own against foreign bikes\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey have a cost advantage and can charge higher prices 几乎照搬男方第6段原话 we\nhave a cost advantage and can charge higher prices，但那是男方在说卖到海外更赚钱\n的理由，跟守国内安全无关，是把别处的原话搬来骗你。\n借用原文：we have a cost advantage and can charge higher prices\nA) 未提及\nThe state subsidizes small and medium enterprises 提到的国家补贴中小企业，全段对\n话从未出现，是凭空添加的内容。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is the man's concern about selling bikes abroad?", "options": ["A) Extra costs might eat up their profits abroad.", "B) More workers will be needed to do packaging.", "C) They might lose to foreign bike manufacturers.", "D) It is very difficult to find suitable local agents."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Extra costs might eat up their profits abroad.\n【定位】But, wait a minute. Packaging, shipping, financing, etc. will push up our costs and we could\nend up no better off, maybe worse off.\n不过，等一下。包装、运输、融资等等会推高我们的成本，我们最后可能没好到哪去，甚至\n更糟。\n【信号】but wait a minute 后的转折顾虑。男方用 But, wait a minute 强转折提醒注意，后面\npush up our costs and we could end up no better off, maybe worse off 就是他的担忧\n落点——转折信号后才是真正的顾虑。\n【替换】push up our costs and we could end up no better off, maybe worse off ↔\nExtra costs might eat up their profits abroad\n成本推高致更糟=吃掉利润\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n录音多处出现 foreign bikes（如 hold our own against foreign bikes），选项 They\nmight lose to foreign bike manufacturers 借这个熟悉对象编出竞争失败的担忧；可男方\n第8段的顾虑是成本，而且双方都觉得能跟外商竞争，并不担心竞争输掉。\n借用原文：I feel sure we could hold our own against foreign bikes\nB) 原词陷阱\n男方确实念到 Packaging 这个词，但他说的是包装会推高成本，不是 More workers will\nbe needed to do packaging 需要更多工人；选项盗用 packaging 一词、嫁接了录音没说\n的加人手结论。\n借用原文：Packaging, shipping, financing, etc. will push up our costs\nD) 未提及\nIt is very difficult to find suitable local agents 说的找本地代理难，全段对话从未涉及代\n理一事，是凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do the speakers agree to do?", "options": ["A) Report to the management.", "B) Attract foreign investments.", "C) Conduct a feasibility study.", "D) Consult financial experts."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Conduct a feasibility study.\n【定位】Um… I'm not that hopeful. But, yes, I think we should go ahead with a feasibility study.\n嗯，我没那么乐观。不过，好吧，我觉得我们应该推进一项可行性研究。\n【信号】but yes 后的拍板句。男方先说 I'm not that hopeful，再用 But, yes, I think we should\ngo ahead with a feasibility study 转折后给出肯定结论——but yes 之后才是双方最终一\n致同意要做的事。\n【替换】I think we should go ahead with a feasibility study ↔ Conduct a feasibility study\ngo ahead with=conduct\n【排除】A) 未提及\nReport to the management 说的向管理层汇报，对话里两人自行拍板，从未提到上报管理\n层，是凭空添加。\nB) 原词陷阱\nAttract foreign investments 借了对话里 foreign、financing 的氛围，但吸引外国投资这\n件事录音从未提，financing 指的是出口环节的融资成本，不是引资。\n借用原文：Packaging, shipping, financing, etc. will push up our costs\nD) 原词陷阱\n女方说过 It's going to need a lot of research，选项 Consult financial experts 蹭了要做\n研究的氛围编成咨询财务专家，但录音从没说要找专家，且双方一致同意的是 feasibility\nstudy，不是咨询专家。\n借用原文：It's going to need a lot of research", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we usually refer to when we talk about energy according to the man?", "options": ["A) Coal burnt daily for the comfort of our homes.", "B) Anything that can be used to produce power.", "C) Fuel refined from oil extracted from underground.", "D) Electricity that keeps all kinds of machines running."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Fuel refined from oil extracted from underground.\n【定位】When we think of energy or fuel for our homes and cars, we think of petroleum—a fossil fuel\nprocessed from oil removed from the ground, of which there is a limited supply.\n当我们想到给家庭和汽车用的能源或燃料时，我们想到的是石油——一种由从地下开采出来\n的油加工而成的化石燃料，而它的储量是有限的。\n【信号】we think of 引出界定。答案信号是男士用 we think of petroleum 把\"谈能源时通常指什\n么\"直接答出来：紧跟其后的 a fossil fuel processed from oil removed from the ground\n就是对\"能源通常指什么\"的具体界定，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】processed from oil removed from the ground ↔ refined from oil extracted from underground\n同义改写：加工＝精炼、地下＝地下\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA Coal burnt daily for the comfort of our homes. 借了原文 for our homes 的 homes\n一词来制造耳熟，但原文说的是 petroleum（石油），不是 coal（煤），答非所问。\n借用原文：energy or fuel for our homes and cars\nB) 张冠李戴\nB Anything that can be used to produce power. 听起来像\"能源\"的泛指定义，但原文里\n能产生动力的\"风、太阳、水\"指的是 alternative fuels（替代燃料），不是题干问的\"谈能源\n时通常指什么\"。把替代能源的范围安到了普通能源头上。\n借用原文：alternative fuels can be many things—wind, sun and water\nD) 未提及\nD Electricity that keeps all kinds of machines running. 把能源说成\"驱动机器的电\"，但\n原文男士界定能源时指的是石油，全程未把它界定为电，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do most experts agree on according to the man?", "options": ["A) Oil will soon be replaced by alternative energy sources.", "B) Oil reserves in the world will be exhausted in a decade.", "C) Oil consumption has given rise to many global problems.", "D) Oil production will begin to decline worldwide by 2025."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Oil production will begin to decline worldwide by 2025.\n【定位】Most experts agree that by around 2025, the amount of petroleum we use will reach a peak.\nThen production and availability will begin to seriously decline.\n多数专家一致认为，到 2025 年前后，我们使用的石油量将达到峰值。此后，石油的产量和\n供应量将开始严重下降。\n【信号】Most experts agree 直接领出答案。答案信号是原词 Most experts agree that——题干\n问的正是\"专家同意什么\"，这句开头就是。紧接的 by around 2025 给出年份、production\nand availability will begin to seriously decline 给出走势，构成完整结论。\n【替换】by around 2025 ↔ by 2025\n年份原样保留\nproduction and availability will begin to seriously decline ↔\nOil production will begin to decline worldwide\ndecline 同词、走势一致\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA Oil will soon be replaced by alternative energy sources. 借了对话反复出现的\nalternative（替代）一词，但原文专家结论说的是石油\"减产\"，从没说石油\"很快被替代\"，\n是听到耳熟词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：alternative fuels can be many things\nB) 与原文相反\nB Oil reserves in the world will be exhausted in a decade. 说\"十年内耗尽\"，但原文明说\nThis is not to say there will be no petroleum at this point.（并不是说那时就没石油\n了），程度被夸大，与原文相反。\n借用原文：This is not to say there will be no petroleum at this point.\nC) 张冠李戴\nC Oil consumption has given rise to many global problems. 像在概括\"全球变暖\"等危\n害，但那是男士回答第8题方向时讲的，不是\"专家同意\"的内容，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：long-term negative effects, like global warming and the greenhouse effect", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man think we should do now?", "options": ["A) Minimize the use of fossil fuels.", "B) Start developing alternative fuels.", "C) Find the real cause for global warming.", "D) Take steps to reduce the greenhouse effect."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Start developing alternative fuels.\n【定位】One is that whether we have 60 or 600 years of fossil fuels left, we have to find other fuel\nsources eventually. So the sooner we start, the better off we will be.\n其一是，不管我们还剩 60 年还是 600 年的化石燃料，我们终究得找到别的燃料来源。所以\n越早动手，我们的处境就越好。\n【信号】have to＋the sooner the better 双重建议。答案信号是必要性词 we have to find other\nfuel sources eventually 加上 the sooner we start, the better off we will be——have\nto 与\"越早越好\"叠在一起，明确主张\"现在就该开始找替代燃料\"，这就是答案句。\n【替换】find other fuel sources ↔ developing alternative fuels\nother fuel sources＝alternative fuels\nthe sooner we start, the better off we will be ↔ Start developing alternative fuels.\nthe sooner we start＝现在就 Start\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA Minimize the use of fossil fuels. 借了原文反复出现的 fossil fuels，但男士主张的是\"去\n找别的燃料\"，并没说要\"减少使用化石燃料\"，听到耳熟词就误选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：whether we have 60 or 600 years of fossil fuels left\nC) 原词陷阱\nC Find the real cause for global warming. 借了原文末尾的 global warming，但原文只\n把全球变暖列为危害，从没主张\"去找它的真正原因\"，张冠李戴／未提及。\n借用原文：like global warming and the greenhouse effect\nD) 原词陷阱\nD Take steps to reduce the greenhouse effect. 借了原文 greenhouse effect，但那是被\n列举的危害，男士主张的是开发替代燃料，不是\"采取措施减温室效应\"，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：global warming and the greenhouse effect", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How do fossil fuels affect the environment?", "options": ["A) They release harmful substances when they are burned.", "B) They dissolve in the water and cause a waste of water.", "C) They release poisonous gas and lead to pollution.", "D) They are burned inefficiently resulting in a waste of resources."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They release harmful substances when they are burned.\n【定位】The other big argument is that when you burn fossil fuels, you release substances trapped in\nthe ground for a long time, which leads to some long-term negative effects, like global\nwarming and the greenhouse effect.\n另一个重要论点是，当你燃烧化石燃料时，你会释放出被长期封存在地下的物质，这会导致\n一些长期的负面影响，比如全球变暖和温室效应。\n【信号】when you burn…you release 讲影响机制。答案信号是描述影响过程的句式 when you\nburn fossil fuels, you release substances——题干问\"如何影响环境\"，这句正给出\"燃烧\n→释放被封存的物质→负面影响\"的机制，是答案句。\n【替换】when you burn fossil fuels ↔ when they are burned\n主动改被动，意思不变\nyou release substances trapped in the ground ↔ They release harmful substances\nsubstances 同词，harmful 概括其负面\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB They dissolve in the water and cause a waste of water. 说化石燃料溶于水、浪费水，\n原文只讲\"释放被封存的物质\"，从没提溶水或浪费水，属未提及。\nC) 过度推断\nC They release poisonous gas and lead to pollution. 用了原文同款 release，但原文只\n笼统说 release substances（释放物质），并没说是\"有毒气体\"、也没说\"污染\"，C 把范围\n悄悄缩小、属过度推断。\n借用原文：you release substances trapped in the ground for a long time\nD) 原词陷阱\nD They are burned inefficiently resulting in a waste of resources. 借了原文的 burn，\n但原文谈的是燃烧释放物质造成的环境危害，从没说\"燃烧低效、浪费资源\"，原词陷阱。\n借用原文：when you burn fossil fuels", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is said to make a good department store buyer?", "options": ["A) The ability to predict fashion trends.", "B) A refined taste for artistic works.", "C) Years of practical experience.", "D) Strict professional training."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The ability to predict fashion trends.\n【定位】They not only have to know what is fashionable at the moment, but also have to guess what\nwill become fashionable next season or next year.\n他们不仅要知道当下什么是流行的，还得猜测下一季或明年什么会变得流行。\n【信号】have to + not only…but also 点明硬性要求。这句用 have to 反复强调 \"必须\"，又用\nnot only…but also 把两项要求并列，是录音里唯一正面交代 \"做这行必须具备什么\" 的句\n子，这种 \"必须/才能\" 的语气就是答案信号。\n【替换】have to guess what will become fashionable next season or next year ↔\nThe ability to predict fashion trends\n\"猜测将来流行什么\"=\"预测流行趋势\"\n【排除】B) 未提及\nA refined taste for artistic works. 看似与 \"采购、商品\" 沾边，但录音通篇只说要懂流行、\n会猜趋势，从没说需要 \"对艺术品的高雅品味\"，属凭话题硬凑的未提及项。\nC) 未提及\nYears of practical experience. 是考生凭常识（干这行当然要经验）容易脑补的，但录音强\n调的是 \"要懂流行、会预测\"，从未提 \"多年实践经验\"，属未提及。\nD) 未提及\nStrict professional training. 同样是常识联想，录音只讲从业者得知道并预测流行，没有任\n何 \"严格专业训练\" 的表述，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does Karen's job involve?", "options": ["A) Promoting all kinds of American hand-made specialties.", "B) Strengthening cooperation with foreign governments.", "C) Conducting trade in art works with dealers overseas.", "D) Purchasing handicrafts from all over the world."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Purchasing handicrafts from all over the world.\n【定位】Her job involves buying handicrafts from all over the world.\n她的工作涉及从世界各地采购手工艺品。\n【信号】job involves 直接点题。录音 Her job involves 与题干 What does Karen's job involve?\n几乎逐词照应，是原文直接交代职责的标志句，听到 involve 这个词即可锁定本句为答\n案句。\n【替换】buying handicrafts from all over the world ↔ Purchasing handicrafts from all over the world\nbuying=Purchasing 的近义替换\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nPromoting all kinds of American hand-made specialties. 盗用了录音里 hand-\nmade/handicrafts 的话题词，却把动作 buying（采购）换成 Promoting（推广）、把范\n围 from all over the world 换成 American，方向和范围全反了，是偷换概念。\n借用原文：buying handicrafts from all over the world\nB) 原词陷阱\nStrengthening cooperation with foreign governments. 盗用了录音 government\noffices that promote handicrafts 里的 government，但录音说的是各国政府办事处 \"乐\n于配合她\"，并非她的工作内容是 \"加强与外国政府合作\"，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：government offices that promote handicrafts\nC) 未提及\nConducting trade in art works with dealers overseas. 用 art works、overseas、\ndealers 制造 \"海外艺术品交易\" 的画面，但录音只说她去各国采购手工艺品，从没提 \"与海\n外商人做艺术品贸易\"，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why does Karen think she has got the best job?", "options": ["A) She has access to fashionable things.", "B) She is doing what she enjoys doing.", "C) She can enjoy life on a modest salary.", "D) She is free to do whatever she wants."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She is doing what she enjoys doing.\n【定位】Karen thinks she has the best job she could have found. She loves all the travelling that she\nhas to do, because she often visits markets and small out-of-the-way places.\n凯伦认为这是她能找到的最好的工作。她热爱自己必须做的所有旅行，因为她常常去逛集市\n和偏僻的小地方。\n【信号】结论句 + 紧随的 loves 与 because。录音先给结论 thinks she has the best job，紧跟着\n用 She loves all the travelling 和 because 给出理由，结论句加感情动词 loves 加因果连\n词 because，三重信号叠加锁定这就是答案句。\n【替换】She loves all the travelling that she has to do ↔ She is doing what she enjoys doing\n\"热爱要做的旅行\"=\"在做自己喜欢的事\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nShe has access to fashionable things. 盗用了录音前文 what is fashionable 的\nfashionable，但那是讲 \"采购员要懂流行\"，不是她觉得工作好的理由；她给的理由是热爱\n旅行，A 答非所问，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：what is fashionable at the moment\nC) 未提及\nShe can enjoy life on a modest salary. 把 \"快乐\" 嫁接到 \"不高的薪水\" 上，但录音通篇没\n出现任何关于工资、收入的内容，是凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nD) 过度推断\nShe is free to do whatever she wants. 用 free、whatever 把意思夸大成 \"想做什么就做\n什么\"，但录音只说她热爱必须做的旅行、能去偏僻地方，并没有说她在工作上 \"自由到为所\n欲为\"，属过度推断。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What did Mark think he should start doing?", "options": ["A) Join in neighborhood patrols.", "B) Get involved in his community.", "C) Voice his complaints to the city council.", "D) Make suggestions to the local authorities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Get involved in his community.\n【定位】Mark smiled and thought, \"Maybe that's a good place to start.\"\n马克笑了，心想：也许这是个不错的起点。\n【信号】结尾点题句 a good place to start。篇章结尾 that's a good place to start 是全文的落点\n——前面铺垫他帮陌生人、读到公交上小小善举会积累起来的海报，这句把这些收束成该从\n这儿开始行动，正是答他要开始做什么的信号句。\n【替换】take part in his community ↔ Get involved in his community\ntake part in 等于 get involved in，同义替换\nthat's a good place to start ↔ Get involved in his community\n结尾该开始呼应开头参与社区\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 neighborhood meeting、Just being neighborly 反复出现 neighbor 一类词，选\n项 Join in neighborhood patrols. 借 neighborhood 这个原词诱选，但 patrols 即巡逻只\n在墙上图表里作为问题之一被提及，他从没说要去参加巡逻。\n借用原文：Police patrols cut back\nC) 原词陷阱\ncity council woman 在录音里出现过，选项 Voice his complaints to the city council. 借\ncity council 原词诱选；但 Mark 结尾是积极地想参与，不是去抱怨即 complaints，方向正\n好相反。\n借用原文：The area city council woman was leading a discussion\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 People were supposed to suggest solutions to the council woman，选项\nMake suggestions to the local authorities. 把向当局提建议这个原文动作搬来诱选；但这\n是会上别人该做的事，Mark 当时反而觉得 this is a waste of my time，结尾他决定的是参\n与社区、不是去提建议。\n借用原文：People were supposed to suggest solutions to the council woman", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What was being discussed when Mark arrived at the neighborhood meeting?", "options": ["A) Deterioration in the quality of life.", "B) Increase of police patrols at night.", "C) Renovation of the vacant buildings.", "D) Violation of community regulations."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Deterioration in the quality of life.\n【定位】The area city council woman was leading a discussion about how the quality of life was on\nthe decline.\n片区的市议会女议员正在主持一场讨论，讨论生活质量如何在不断下滑。\n【信号】话题引出句 a discussion about。leading a discussion about how the quality of life\nwas on the decline 里 discussion about 直接点出讨论的主题就是生活质量在下滑，是回\n答在讨论什么的信号句。\n【替换】the quality of life was on the decline ↔ Deterioration in the quality of life\non the decline 等于 Deterioration，名词化替换\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音图表里念到 Police patrols cut back 即巡逻被削减，选项 Increase of police patrols\nat night. 借 police patrols 原词诱选，却把 cut back 即削减反成 Increase 即增加，极性\n正好相反，而且 patrols 只是墙上诸多问题之一，不是讨论主题。\n借用原文：Police patrols cut back\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 problems in vacant buildings 出现过 vacant buildings，选项 Renovation of the\nvacant buildings. 借这个原词诱选；但录音只说空楼有问题，从没提 Renovation 即翻修，\n且它只是图表上的一项，不是会议讨论主题。\n借用原文：problems in vacant buildings\nD) 未提及\n选项 Violation of community regulations. 说的违反社区规定，录音通篇没出现\nregulations 或违规一说，属凭空捏造，与讨论主题无关。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did Mark think of the community's problems?", "options": ["A) They may take a long time to solve.", "B) They need assistance from the city.", "C) They have to be dealt with one by one.", "D) They are too big for individual efforts."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They are too big for individual efforts.\n【定位】It was too much for Mark. \"The problems are too big,\" he thought.\n这对马克来说太难承受了。这些问题太大了，他心想。\n【信号】主观评价句 too much 与 too big。The problems are too big 是 Mark 直接说出的判\n断，紧接着 Nothing I could do would make a difference here 即我做什么都改变不了，\n补足了太大、个人无能为力的态度，是答他看法的信号句。\n【替换】The problems are too big ↔ They are too big for individual efforts\ntoo big 原意保留，补出个人办不了\nNothing I could do would make a difference here ↔ for individual efforts\n我个人改变不了等于超出个人之力\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 They may take a long time to solve. 即要花很久才能解决听上去合理，但 Mark 的\n原话只说问题太大、个人无力，从没提及耗时长，是顺着常识推过头、录音无据。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 city council woman 出现过 city，选项 They need assistance from the city. 借\ncity 原词诱选；但 Mark 的态度是太大、自己没用，从没说需要市里援助，把熟词拼了个录\n音没有的看法。\n借用原文：The area city council woman\nC) 未提及\n选项 They have to be dealt with one by one. 即得一个个处理，录音里既无 one by one\n之意、Mark 也没表达过这种看法，纯属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "Why did Mark smile on his ride home?", "options": ["A) He had got some groceries at a big discount.", "B) He had read a funny poster near his seat.", "C) He had done a small deed of kindness.", "D) He had caught the bus just in time."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He had done a small deed of kindness.\n【定位】Then he picked up the stray groceries while the woman smiled in relief. \"Just being\nneighborly,\" Mark said. As he rode home, he glanced at the poster near his seat in the bus:\nSmall acts of kindness add up.\n然后他捡起散落的杂货，那位女士如释重负地笑了。只是做个好邻居罢了，马克说。回家路\n上，他瞥见座位旁的海报：小小的善举会积累起来。\n【信号】微笑前的善举情节加点睛海报。他笑之前刚做了好事：picked up the stray groceries 并说\nJust being neighborly，又读到海报 Small acts of kindness add up；正是这件善举加上\n海报让他释然微笑，这段情节就是答原因的信号。\n【替换】Just being neighborly ↔ He had done a small deed of kindness\n做个好邻居等于做了件善事，详述到概括\nSmall acts of kindness add up ↔ a small deed of kindness\nacts of kindness 与 deed of kindness 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 groceries started to fall out、picked up the stray groceries 反复出现 groceries，\n选项 He had got some groceries at a big discount. 借这个高频原词诱选；但那是别人即\n那位女士的杂货、是 Mark 帮忙捡的，根本不是他打折买到的，big discount 纯属捏造。\n借用原文：he picked up the stray groceries\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 glanced at the poster near his seat，选项 He had read a funny poster near\nhis seat. 借 poster near his seat 原词诱选；但海报内容是 Small acts of kindness add\nup 即小善举会积累，是发人深省、不是 funny 即搞笑，把性质偷换了。\n借用原文：he glanced at the poster near his seat in the bus\nD) 偷换概念\n录音里那位女士夸他 You've got great timing 即你来得真及时，选项 He had caught the\nbus just in time. 借 timing 这层意思诱选；但 great timing 指他及时救了孩子，不是刚好\n赶上公交，他微笑是为善举，不是赶上车。\n借用原文：You've got great timing", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is the talk mainly about?", "options": ["A) The difficulties of industrialization in North America.", "B) The influence of industrialization on people's life.", "C) The negative effect of industrialization in North America.", "D) Improved ways of organizing the manufacturing of goods."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Improved ways of organizing the manufacturing of goods.\n【定位】Industrialization came to the United States after 1790 as North American entrepreneurs\nincreased productivity by reorganizing work and building factories. These innovations in\nmanufacturing boosted output and living standards to an unprecedented extent.\n1790 年后工业化来到美国，北美的企业家通过重组工作、建立工厂提高了生产率。制造业\n的这些革新把产量和生活水平提升到了前所未有的程度。\n【信号】开头点题句。篇章独白的主旨往往落在开头：首句 increased productivity by\nreorganizing work and building factories 就点出全篇主线——靠\"重组工作、建工厂\"这\n种更好的生产组织方式来提产量，后面两段（鞋业工作体系、工厂机器）都在展开这条\n主线。\n【替换】reorganizing work and building factories ↔\nImproved ways of organizing the manufacturing of goods\n概括式同义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe difficulties of industrialization in North America. 说工业化的\"困难\"，但全篇只讲工\n业化带来的成就（产量、生活水平上升），从未讲它有多难，difficulties 是凭 North\nAmerica 这个原词制造的话题陷阱，内容未提及。\n借用原文：Industrialization came to the United States\nB) 偷换概念\nThe influence of industrialization on people's life. 抓的是 boosted output and living\nstandards 里\"生活水平\"那半句，但\"对生活的影响\"只是结果之一，全篇主线是\"如何改进生\n产方式\"，把一个支线结果当主旨，范围偏小。\n借用原文：boosted output and living standards\nC) 与原文相反\nThe negative effect of industrialization in North America. 强调\"负面影响\"，但全篇基调\n是正面的（产量提升、生活变好）；唯一沾点负面的 eroded workers' control 只是鞋业一\n段里的一句，远不足以成为主旨，方向与全篇相反。\n借用原文：eroded workers' control over the pace and conditions of labor", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "Why does the speaker mention the shoe industry?", "options": ["A) To provide an example of how entrepreneurs increased output by using an extended work system.", "B) To provide an example of how entrepreneurs used technological improvements to increase output.", "C) To provide an example of how rural workers responded to shoe bosses.", "D) To provide an example of how changes in the work system improved the quality of shoes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nTo provide an example of how entrepreneurs increased output by using an extended work\nsystem.\n【定位】Since the 1790's, North American entrepreneurs—even without technological improvements\n—had broadened the scope of the work system that made manufacturing more efficient by\ndistributing materials to a succession of workers who each performed a single step of the\nproduction process. For example, during the 1820's and 1830's the shoe industry greatly\nexpanded the scale and extent of the work system.\n自 1790 年代起，北美企业家——即便没有技术改进——也扩展了工作体系的范围，这种体\n系把材料分发给一连串各自只完成生产过程中某一步的工人，从而让制造更有效率。例如，\n在 1820、1830 年代，鞋业大大扩大了工作体系的规模和范围。\n【信号】For example 前的论点句。举例信号 For example 紧跟在论点句 had broadened the\nscope of the work system 之后——鞋业这个例子正是为了印证\"企业家在没有技术改进的\n情况下、靠扩展工作体系提高产量\"这个论点，论点句才是答案落点。\n【替换】had broadened the scope of the work system ↔ using an extended work system\n同义改写\neven without technological improvements ↔\nincreased output by using an extended work system\n排除技术、坐实工作体系\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nused technological improvements to increase output 盗用了原文 technological\nimprovements 这个原词，但录音原话是 even without technological improvements\n——恰恰是\"没有技术改进\"，B 把\"没有技术\"偷换成\"靠技术\"，正好说反。\n借用原文：even without technological improvements\nC) 原词陷阱\nhow rural workers responded to shoe bosses 拼接了原文的 rural women、shoe boss\n等原词，但原文只说这套体系 made the employer a powerful shoe boss、工人被动失去\n控制，从没讲工人如何\"回应\"老板；这是借原词制造的答非所问。\n借用原文：made the employer a powerful shoe boss\nD) 偷换概念\nimproved the quality of shoes 借了原文 shoes，但原文讲的是工作体系 increased the\noutput of shoes while cutting the price（增产、降价），是\"数量与价格\"，从未说提高\n了\"质量\"，质量是无中生有。\n借用原文：increased the output of shoes while cutting the price", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "According to the talk, how did later mills differ from the mill built by Oliver Evans?", "options": ["A) They were located away from large cities.", "B) They used new technology to produce power.", "C) They did not allow flour to cool before it was placed in barrels.", "D) They combined technology with the work system."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They used new technology to produce power.\n【定位】As early as 1782 the creative inventor Oliver Evans had built a highly automated, laborsaving\nflour mill driven by water power. Subsequently, manufacturers made use of new improved\nstationary steam engines to power their mills.\n早在 1782 年，富有创造力的发明家 Oliver Evans 就建了一座高度自动化、省力的面粉厂，\n由水力驱动。后来，制造商们利用新改良的固定式蒸汽机来为他们的工厂提供动力。\n【信号】Subsequently 引出的对比。转折/递进信号 Subsequently（后来）把两个对象的动力来源\n摆出对比：Evans 那座 driven by water power（水力），后来的工厂 new improved\nstationary steam engines（蒸汽机），差异点就在\"动力来源\"。\n【替换】new improved stationary steam engines to power their mills ↔\nused new technology to produce power\n具体到概括的同义替换\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey were located away from large cities. 说后来工厂\"远离大城市\"，但原文恰说蒸汽机\n让他们 build factories in the nation's largest cities（建在最大城市），away from cities\n与原文正相反。\n借用原文：build factories in the nation's largest cities\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey did not allow flour to cool before it was placed in barrels. 拼了原文 flour、\nbarrels、cool 等原词，但原文讲的是 Evans 的机器 allow it to cool as it descended into\nbarrels（让面粉冷却后入桶），这是 Evans 那座厂的特征、且是\"让其冷却\"，C 既张冠李戴\n又把\"让冷却\"说成\"不让冷却\"。\n借用原文：conveyed the flour back to the top of the mill to allow it to cool as it descended\ninto barrels\nD) 偷换概念\nThey combined technology with the work system. 把第3段的 technology 与第2段的\nwork system 两个原词拼到一起，但原文从未说后来工厂\"把技术与工作体系结合\"，第3段\n只讲蒸汽机供能，结合之说属无中生有。\n借用原文：broadened the scope of the work system", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "Which of the following is mentioned as a result of improvements in factory machinery?", "options": ["A) It became easier for factory owners to find workers and customers.", "B) Manufacturers had to employ more highly skilled workers.", "C) The amount of power required for factories' operation was reduced.", "D) Factories could operate more than one engine at a time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It became easier for factory owners to find workers and customers.\n【定位】This new technology enabled them to build factories in the nation's largest cities, taking\nadvantage of urban inexpensive labor, good transportation networks, and eager customers.\n这项新技术使他们得以在全国最大的城市建厂，从而利用城市里廉价的劳动力、良好的交通\n网络和热切的顾客。\n【信号】因果词 enabled。因果信号 This new technology enabled them（这项新技术使他们\n得以……）直接把\"机器改进\"与其结果挂上：结果是进城建厂，进而 taking advantage\nof urban inexpensive labor…and eager customers——更易得到廉价劳力（工人）与\n顾客。\n【替换】urban inexpensive labor ↔ to find workers\n下位到上位的替换\neager customers ↔ and customers\n原义同指\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nManufacturers had to employ more highly skilled workers. 说要雇\"更熟练的工人\"，但\n原文是工厂进城利用 inexpensive labor（廉价劳力），且工作体系本就在削减对 skilled\nshoemakers 的依赖，方向与\"更熟练工人\"相反。\n借用原文：urban inexpensive labor\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe amount of power required for factories' operation was reduced. 借了原文反复出\n现的 power，但原文只说蒸汽机 to power their mills（供能），从没说运转所需动力\"减少\n了\"，reduced 是无中生有。\n借用原文：steam engines to power their mills\nD) 未提及\nFactories could operate more than one engine at a time. 借了原文 steam engines 的\nengine，但原文从未提\"同时开多台引擎\"，more than one engine 是凭原词臆造的细节。\n借用原文：new improved stationary steam engines", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "According to the talk, what was a highly significant factor in the development of realist and naturalist literature?", "options": ["A) The Civil War.", "B) An economic depression.", "C) A recognition that romanticism was unpopular.", "D) An increased interest in the study of common speech."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） An economic depression.\n【定位】The economic depression in the late-nineteenth-century United States contributed\nsignificantly to a growing movement in literature toward realism and naturalism.\n十九世纪后期美国的经济萧条，对文学中走向现实主义和自然主义的日益壮大的潮流起了重\n大的推动作用。\n【信号】因果信号 contributed to。答案句靠因果动词锁定：contributed significantly to a\ngrowing movement in literature toward realism and naturalism，contributed to 直接\n点出 谁推动了现实主义与自然主义，前面的 The economic depression 就是那个因素。\n【替换】The economic depression ↔ An economic depression.\n近乎同形的概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念了 the Civil War of 1861-1865，但它只是用来标记浪漫主义流行的时间段，并\n非推动现实主义的因素，选项盗这个原词诱你 听到就选。\n借用原文：the Civil War of 1861-1865\nC) 未提及\n录音说作家们 began to reject the romanticism（主动拒绝浪漫主义），并未说 浪漫主义\n不受欢迎被公认；A recognition that romanticism was unpopular. 把 作家主动拒绝 偷换\n成 大众公认其不流行，原文未提。\n借用原文：began to reject the romanticism\nD) 张冠李戴\nAn increased interest in the study of common speech. 里 common speech 是后文讲\n马克·吐温时才出现的 common speech，被挪来当现实主义的成因，既张冠又李戴，并非\n第 20 题答案。\n借用原文：used dialect and common speech", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "Why is Mark Twain considered as a very important literary figure?", "options": ["A) Because he wrote humorous stories and novels.", "B) Because he rejected romanticism as a literary approach.", "C) Because he was the first realist writer in the United States.", "D) Because he influenced American prose style through his use of common speech."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\nBecause he influenced American prose style through his use of common speech.\n【定位】In his stories and novels, Twain drew on his own experiences and used dialect and common\nspeech instead of literary language, touching off a major change in American prose style.\n在他的故事和小说里，吐温取材于自己的亲身经历，用方言和日常口语而非文学化语言，由\n此引发了美国散文文体的一场重大变革。\n【信号】结果信号 touching off a major change。答案句靠 引发重大变革 这一结果短语锁定：\nused dialect and common speech instead of literary language, touching off a major\nchange in American prose style 直接点出吐温的历史贡献——用口语改变了散文文体。\n【替换】used dialect and common speech instead of literary language ↔\nthrough his use of common speech\n原文细节被概括\ntouching off a major change in American prose style ↔ influenced American prose style\n引发变革=施加影响\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确说吐温 observing life around him with a humorous and skeptical eye，\nhumorous 是真念过的词，但它形容观察眼光，不是他 重要 的理由；he wrote humorous\nstories and novels 借这个原词诱选。\n借用原文：a humorous and skeptical eye\nB) 张冠李戴\n拒绝浪漫主义说的是第 1 段那批作家 began to reject the romanticism，录音并未说吐温\n本人 rejected romanticism；he rejected romanticism as a literary approach 把前文群\n体的行为安到吐温头上。\n借用原文：began to reject the romanticism\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说吐温是 the country's most outstanding realist author（最杰出的现实主义作\n家），并未说他是 第一个现实主义作家；he was the first realist writer in the United\nStates 把 最杰出 偷换成 第一个，原文无据。\n借用原文：the country's most outstanding realist author", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What can we learn about Theodore Dreiser, according to the talk?", "options": ["A) He mainly wrote about historical subjects such as the Civil War.", "B) His novels often contained elements of humor.", "C) He viewed himself more as a social observer than as a literary artist.", "D) He believed writers should emphasize the positive aspects of life."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He viewed himself more as a social observer than as a literary artist.\n【定位】Dreiser thought that writers should tell the truth about human affairs, not fabricate romance,\nand Sister Carrie, he said, was not intended as a piece of literary craftsmanship, but was a\npicture of conditions.\n德莱塞认为作家应当如实讲述人间世事，而不是编造浪漫故事；他说，《嘉莉妹妹》并非要\n作为一件文学技艺的作品，而是一幅对（社会）现状的写照。\n【信号】对比信号 not A but B。答案句靠两处 not…but… 对比锁定：not intended as a piece of\nliterary craftsmanship, but was a picture of conditions，否定 文学技艺、肯定 反映现\n状，正是德莱塞的自我定位落点。\n【替换】a picture of conditions ↔ as a social observer\n社会写照=社会观察者\nnot intended as a piece of literary craftsmanship ↔ than as a literary artist\n非文学技艺=非文学艺术家\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nCivil War 是第 1 段交代年代时念的 the Civil War of 1861-1865，与德莱塞无关；He\nmainly wrote about historical subjects such as the Civil War. 搬这个醒目原词硬编德莱\n塞的题材，原文从未这样说。\n借用原文：the Civil War of 1861-1865\nB) 张冠李戴\n幽默说的是吐温 a humorous and skeptical eye，录音对德莱塞用的是 grimly portrayed\na dark world（冷峻地描绘黑暗世界）；His novels often contained elements of humor.\n把吐温的幽默错安到德莱塞身上，方向还相反。\n借用原文：a humorous and skeptical eye\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说德莱塞 grimly portrayed a dark world 并主张 tell the truth about human\naffairs, not fabricate romance，强调如实写阴暗现实，恰与 writers should emphasize\nthe positive aspects of life（强调生活积极面）相反。\n借用原文：grimly portrayed a dark world", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does Dr. Hudson say regarding the vast majority of species in Earth's history at the beginning of the lecture?", "options": ["A) They have remained basically unchanged from their original forms.", "B) They have been able to adapt to ecological changes.", "C) They have caused rapid change in the environment.", "D) They are no longer in existence."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are no longer in existence.\n【定位】It is estimated that over 99 percent of all species that ever existed have become extinct.\n据估计，曾经存在过的所有物种中，超过 99% 已经灭绝。\n【信号】开头比例数字＝定调句。信号是开头的高比例数字 over 99 percent of all species that\never existed have become extinct——讲座一上来就用 99% 这个数字给\"绝大多数物\n种\"定性，正对题干 the vast majority 与 at the beginning。\n【替换】over 99 percent of all species that ever existed have become extinct ↔\nThey are no longer in existence.\n已灭绝＝不复存在\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nremained basically unchanged from their original forms 说物种\"基本没变\"，与原文\nhave become extinct（已灭绝）正好相反；绝大多数物种是消失了，不是原样没变。\n借用原文：have become extinct\nB) 原词陷阱\nadapt to ecological changes 盗用了讲座反复出现的 adapt（适应）一词，听到\"适应\"就\n想选；但原文说\"绝大多数物种已灭绝\"，恰恰是\"未能适应\"，且 adapt 是后文讲灭绝成因时\n才提，答非\"绝大多数物种现状\"之问。\n借用原文：adapted\nC) 偷换概念\ncaused rapid change in the environment 把因果说反了：原文是\"环境的剧变\"导致物种\n灭绝（Rapid ecological change may render an environment hostile to a species），\n而非物种造成了环境剧变。\n借用原文：Rapid ecological change may render an environment hostile to a species", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the evidence from fossils suggest?", "options": ["A) Extinction of species has occurred from time to time throughout Earth's history.", "B) Extinctions on Earth have generally been massive.", "C) There has been only one mass extinction in Earth's history.", "D) Dinosaurs became extinct much earlier than scientists originally believed."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nExtinction of species has occurred from time to time throughout Earth's history.\n【定位】The fossil record reveals that extinction has occurred throughout the history of Earth.\n化石记录揭示，灭绝在地球的整个历史中一直发生着。\n【信号】信息源词fossil领出答案句。信号是题干点名的信息源词在原文现身：The fossil record\nreveals that extinction has occurred throughout the history of Earth——fossil record\n一出现，紧跟的 reveals that… 就是它\"显示了什么\"的答案句。\n【替换】extinction has occurred throughout the history of Earth ↔\nExtinction of species has occurred from time to time throughout Earth's history\n贯穿历史＝时有发生\n【排除】B) 过度推断\ngenerally been massive 用 generally（普遍）把灭绝一概说成大规模，盗用了下文 a\nmass extinction 的 mass 一词；但原文只说\"某些时候\"出现大灭绝（on some occasions\nmany species become extinct at the same time），并非\"普遍都是\"，属以偏概全。\n借用原文：on some occasions many species become extinct at the same time\nC) 与原文相反\nonly one mass extinction 说\"只有一次\"大灭绝，盗用了原文 mass extinction 原词；但原\n文明确举了多次（65 million years ago 恐龙、225 million years ago 95%物种），One\nof the best-known examples 的 one of（之一）已表明不止一次，与 only one 相反。\n借用原文：One of the best-known examples of mass extinction\nD) 原词陷阱\nDinosaurs became extinct much earlier than scientists originally believed 盗用了原文\n念到的 dinosaurs（恐龙），听到\"恐龙\"就想选；但原文只说恐龙灭绝发生在 65 million\nyears ago，是举例说明大灭绝，根本没说\"比科学家原先认为的更早\"，属无中生有。\n借用原文：65 million years ago with the demise of dinosaurs", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What can we learn about the \"interesting and controversial\" finding?", "options": ["A) Extinctions during the past 25 million years have tended to be more intense every 2.6 million years.", "B) The theory that the periodic extinction had something to do with the Earth's movement is only a speculation.", "C) This finding was already confirmed by the most prestigious scientists in the world.", "D) A species' survival totally depends on its ability or inability to adapt."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\nThe theory that the periodic extinction had something to do with the Earth's movement is only\na speculation.\n【定位】This periodic extinction might be due to intersection of the Earth's orbit with a cloud of\ncomets, but this theory is purely speculative.\n这种周期性灭绝可能源于地球轨道与一团彗星的交汇，但这一理论纯属推测。\n【信号】转折but后才是定性落点。信号是转折词 but：前半 This periodic extinction might be\ndue to intersection of the Earth's orbit with a cloud of comets 只是抛出一个可能解\n释，but this theory is purely speculative 的转折之后才给出真正定性——这理论纯属推\n测。篇章题的结论常落在 but 之后。\n【替换】but this theory is purely speculative ↔\nThe theory that the periodic extinction had something to do with the Earth's movement is only\na speculation.\n纯属推测＝只是猜测\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nmore intense every 2.6 million years 盗用了原文\"周期\"这一说法和\"more intense\"的措\n辞，但把数字偷改了：原文是 more intense every 26 million years（每2600万年），选\n项写成 every 2.6 million years（每260万年），小数点位置不符，是数字陷阱。\n借用原文：more intense every 26 million years\nC) 与原文相反\nalready confirmed by the most prestigious scientists 说该发现\"已被最权威科学家证\n实\"，与原文 purely speculative（纯属推测）正好相反，且 most prestigious 是原文完全\n没有的拔高，属凭空加码。\n借用原文：this theory is purely speculative\nD) 与原文相反\nA species' survival totally depends on its ability or inability to adapt. 盗用了原文\nability or inability to adapt 的措辞，但原文恰恰说 A species' survival may have\nnothing to do with its ability or inability to adapt（可能毫无关系），选项的 totally\ndepends（完全取决于）与之极性相反。\n借用原文：A species' survival may have nothing to do with its ability or inability to adapt", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) swept\n动词\n【词性槽】were + 空 + aside = 被动语态的过去分词位\n【依据】be swept aside 即被推到一边、被淘汰，是固定搭配\nhand weavers were __(26)__ aside by the mechanical loom.——sweep aside 是英文里\n把人/事物推到一边、抛弃/淘汰的固定动词短语，与本段 Innovation has always cost\npeople their jobs 的主旨完美吻合。\n【竞争词】B) displaced displaced 不与 aside 搭配\nbe displaced 表被取代/被迫迁移，可独立使用，但不与 aside 构成短语；本句紧跟 aside，搭\n配上必须是能与 aside 连用的动词。displaced 留给 27 题。\nO) withdrawn be withdrawn aside 不成立\nwithdrawn 表被撤回，与 aside 既无固定搭配，也无法表达被机械织布机淘汰的含义。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— displaced 中文 取代 看着也对，但搭配错。hand weavers\nwere __(26)__ aside by the mechanical loom. 一句考生很容易直接想到取代 →\ndisplaced；但 displaced 不与 aside 搭配，be swept aside 才是固定短语。防坑：动词后\n跟 aside/off/up 等小品词时，先想短语搭配，再回头看中文意思。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) displaced\n动词\n【词性槽】has + 空 + 宾语 = 现在完成时及物动词\n【依据】displace jobs 即取代岗位，对应下文 just as the weavers were\nTypists, ticket agents, bank tellers and many production-line jobs have been\ndispensed with, just as the weavers were.——下句以 just as the weavers were 类比\n26 题织工被淘汰的命运，本句即数字革命取代了这些中等技能岗位，displace 正是这种取\n代关系的标准动词。\n【竞争词】O) withdrawn withdraw 不能直接取代岗位\nhas withdrawn many of the mid-skill jobs 字面是撤回了这些岗位，方向错——岗位不是被撤\n回，而是被取代消失；withdraw 通常用于撤资、撤兵、撤回声明等。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) prosperity\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 rising 后缺名词中心词\n【依据】rising 与 prosperity 是常见搭配，且呼应下句 richer\nsuch disruption is a natural part of rising __(28)__. Although innovation kills some\njobs, it creates new and better ones, as a more __(29)__ society becomes richer——\n下一句 becomes richer 直接给出语义提示：上升的应是繁荣 prosperity，rising\nprosperity 也是固定搭配。\n【竞争词】A) benefits 复数搭配不顺、且后面要留给技术之 benefits\nrising benefits 可勉强讲通，但本段强调的是社会整体的繁荣上升，不是某项具体好处的增加；\n且 benefits 一词在后文 33 题处对应 its __(33)__.，更契合那里的语境。\nE) impact 意思偏向冲击影响、与后文 richer 不呼应\nrising impact 指影响越来越大，与本句要表达的整体社会向上变好不匹配；impact 也更适合留\n给 34 题 Technology's __(34)__。\nK) rhythm 节奏与社会向好无关\nrhythm 表节奏/韵律，rising rhythm 既不是常见搭配，也无法呼应下句的 richer\n与 wealthier。\nL) sentiments 情绪可升可降但与全段经济变好无关\nrising sentiments 多用于情绪/情感语境，本段在讲社会更富裕、更有产出，sentiments 偏离\n主线。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) productive\n形容词\n【词性槽】more 与 society 之间需要一个形容词\n【依据】productive society 直接对应富裕、丰产的论调\nas a more __(29)__ society becomes richer and its wealthier inhabitants demand\nmore goods and services.——社会越富裕，居民越有钱买更多商品和服务，前因正是社\n会更具生产力（more productive）；下文 Today less than 2% of them produce far\nmore food. 也是 productive 的具体例证。\n【竞争词】J) responsive 反应敏捷与生产力提升无关\nresponsive 强调反应/回应（多用于 responsive to sth.），无法解释为何社会变富、产能上\n升；且 a more responsive society becomes richer 在逻辑上断链。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) employed\n动词\n【词性槽】was 后缺过去分词构成被动\n【依据】be employed on a farm 即在农场就业，是固定表达\nA hundred years ago one in three American workers was __(30)__ on a farm.——三分\n之一的美国劳动者一百年前在农场上工作（被雇佣），be employed on/in 是表示就业领\n域的固定搭配，且与下句 The millions freed from the land（从土地上解放出来的数百万\n人）形成强呼应：先在农场上工作，后来才被解放。\n【竞争词】M) shrunk 主语是工人、不能被缩小\nbe shrunk 用于体积/规模收缩的主语，workers 这种人的集合不能 be shrunk on a farm，语\n义不通。\nN) swept 被席卷与就业无关、且与 35 题语境冲突\nwas swept on a farm 既无法表达就业意思，又与第 35 题处的 __(35)__ sweeping through\npoorer countries too. 语境冲突——sweeping 一词已经在 35 题附近出现过含义对应。\nO) withdrawn 撤回不能修饰在农场工作\nbe withdrawn 指被撤回/撤出，与 on a farm 搭配讲不通，逻辑反向。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) jobless\n形容词\n【词性槽】render + 宾语 + 形容词 中的形容词位\n【依据】but 转折锁定前后语义相反\nwere not rendered __(31)__, but found better-paid work——but 后是找到了更好的工\n作，按 not...but... 的对立结构，前半句否定的应是失业状态：not rendered jobless 即没\n有被搞成失业，逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】J) responsive 反应敏捷与失业语境无关\nbe rendered responsive（被弄得反应敏捷）与上下文从土地解放后是否就业的话题无关，且不\n能与 but found better-paid work 形成对立。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) shrunk\n动词\n【词性槽】has + 空 = 现在完成时谓语缺过去分词\n【依据】但 but 后是变多，前半句应表达变少 → shrunk\nToday the pool of secretaries has __(32)__, but there are ever more computer\nprogrammers and web designers.——but 后是程序员和网页设计师变得越来越多，按\nbut 的对立逻辑，前半句秘书的队伍应是缩小：has shrunk 指缩水，与 the pool of\nsecretaries（秘书的人才池）搭配自然。\n【竞争词】B) displaced displaced 一般主语应是动作发出者或被取代的具体职位\nthe pool has displaced 字面是池子取代了什么，搭配不顺；且 displaced 更契合 26 题 hand\nweavers 被取代的语境，与本句要表达的数量收缩不符。\nN) swept 已被席卷与池子收缩语义不合\nthe pool has swept 表池子席卷了某物，方向反了；且 swept 更契合 26 题 hand weavers\nwere swept aside 的固定表达。\nO) withdrawn withdrawn 多用于撤回/撤资/撤军\nthe pool has withdrawn（人才池撤回了）语义不通，且 withdraw 通常需要明确的撤回对象。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) benefits\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词性物主代词 its 后缺名词\n【依据】对立结构 dislocating effects vs benefits\nthe dislocating effects of technology may make themselves evident faster than its\n__(33)__.——句中 dislocating effects（造成混乱的负面影响）与 its + 空 构成正负面对\n立：技术的负面冲击显现得比其好处更快。benefits 正是 dislocating effects 的反向对\n照，且呼应下句 Even if new jobs and wonderful products emerge——这些新岗位和好\n产品就是 benefits 的具体化。\n【竞争词】E) impact impact 是中性冲击，与 dislocating effects 重合非对立\nits impact 与 the dislocating effects of technology 几乎同义，构不成 faster than 的对照；\n且 impact 留给 34 题 Technology's __(34)__。\nK) rhythm 节奏与好处无关\nits rhythm 表技术的节奏，与本句要表达的正面/负面对照无关。\nL) sentiments 技术不带情绪\ntechnology's sentiments 在英文里几乎不成立，技术本身不具备情感。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— impact 同为名词且语义模糊，最易抢答。faster than its\n__(33)__ 一句里，its impact 字面上和 the dislocating effects of technology 都能扯上关\n系，考生很容易抢填 E；但 faster than 要求两侧对照，impact 与 dislocating effects 是\n同义而非对照，benefits 才是正面反向。防坑：见 faster than/more than 先想正反对照。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) impact\n名词\n【词性槽】Technology's 所有格后缺名词中心词\n【依据】impact 与 tornado/hitting 同属冲击意象\nTechnology's __(34)__ will feel like a tornado(旋风), hitting the rich world first, but\n__(35)__ sweeping through poorer countries too.——比喻成旋风、动词用\nhitting/sweeping，全部是冲击/席卷的意象，主语必是 impact（冲击）。\n【竞争词】K) rhythm 节奏与旋风/重击意象不符\ntechnology's rhythm 节奏感平稳，与 tornado、hitting 这些猛烈意象不匹配。\nL) sentiments 技术不带情绪，且不会像旋风\ntechnology's sentiments 几乎不成立，且情绪也不会 hit the rich world，意象不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) benefits", "B) displaced", "C) employed", "D) eventually", "E) impact", "F) jobless", "G) primarily", "H) productive", "I) prosperity", "J) responsive", "K) rhythm", "L) sentiments", "M) shrunk", "N) swept", "O) withdrawn"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) eventually\n副词\n【词性槽】but 后、分词 sweeping 前需要副词修饰\n【依据】与 first 形成 先…后… 的时间对照\nhitting the rich world first, but __(35)__ sweeping through poorer countries too.——\n前半句 first（先击中富国），but 后需要一个表示之后/最终的副词与 first 对照，\neventually 正是最终/终究的时间副词，构成 先击富国、终席卷穷国 的清晰时序。\n【竞争词】G) primarily primarily 表主要地、不表时间顺序\nprimarily 强调主次（主要是…），无法与 first 形成时间先后的对比，且 but primarily\nsweeping 在逻辑上反而像在自我否定前半句（先击富国但主要是席卷穷国），与全句意思\n矛盾。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to Duncan Watts, the superiority of the \"Mona Lisa\" to Leonardo's other works resulted from the cumulative advantage.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Duncan Watts + 累积优势 + 达·芬奇其他作品\n【定位】The process described by Cutting evokes a principle that the sociologist Duncan Watts calls\n\"cumulative advantage\": once a thing becomes popular, it will tend to become more popular\nstill. ... why was it considered so superior to the three other Leonardos in the previous\nchamber, to which nobody seemed to be paying the slightest attention?\nCutting 所描述的过程引出了社会学家 Duncan Watts 所说的「累积优势」原则：一旦某\n物变得受欢迎，它会变得更加受欢迎……（在卢浮宫排队看完《蒙娜丽莎》之后他困惑地\n走开：）为什么这幅画被认为远胜于隔壁展厅里那三幅达·芬奇作品——而那三幅几乎无人\n理睬？\n【改写】According to Duncan Watts ↔ the sociologist Duncan Watts calls\n同一人引语的同义复述\nthe cumulative advantage ↔ \"cumulative advantage\"\n术语原样复现\nthe superiority of the \"Mona Lisa\" to Leonardo's other works ↔\nwhy was it considered so superior to the three other Leonardos in the previous chamber\n反问句被陈述化、具体被上位概括\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然也由 Watts 发声，Duncan Watts proposes that the \"Mona Lisa\" is\nmerely an extreme example of a general rule，但落点是「随机事件/偏好掀起的影\n响波」，并未提到 cumulative advantage 这一术语，也未触及「对 Leonardo 其他\n作品的优越性」。\nDuncan Watts proposes that the \"Mona Lisa\" is merely an extreme example of a general rule", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Some social scientists have raised doubts about the intrinsic value of certain works of art.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】社会科学家 + 内在价值 的疑问\n【定位】But some social scientists have been asking awkward questions of it, raising the possibility\nthat artistic canons(名作目录) are little more than fossilised historical accidents.\n但有些社会科学家一直在对这种解释提出令人尴尬的问题，并提出一种可能：所谓名作目录\n不过是被定型化的历史偶然。\n【改写】Some social scientists ↔ some social scientists\n身份词原样复现\nhave raised doubts ↔ have been asking awkward questions of it\n提出尴尬问题=表达怀疑\nthe intrinsic value of certain works of art ↔\nsome works of art are just great: of intrinsically superior quality\nintrinsic value=intrinsically superior quality\n【辨邻】 K K 段确实提到 intrinsic quality，并承认 The intrinsic quality of a work of art is\nstarting to seem like its least important attribute，但紧接着话锋一转，But\nperhaps it's more significant than our social scientists allow——这是作者本人反\n过来为内在价值辩护，与题干「社会科学家提出怀疑」的主体和立场都不符。\nBut perhaps it's more significant than our social scientists allow", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is often random events or preferences that determine the fate of a piece of art.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】随机事件/偏好 决定艺术命运\n【定位】Paintings, poems and pop songs are buoyed(使浮起) or sunk by random events or preferences\nthat turn into waves of influence, passing down the generations.\n绘画、诗歌和流行歌曲都被一些随机事件或偏好托起或淹没——这些事件和偏好转化为代代\n相传的影响浪潮。\n【改写】random events or preferences ↔ random events or preferences\n整短语原样复现\ndetermine the fate of a piece of art ↔\nPaintings, poems and pop songs are buoyed(使浮起) or sunk\n比喻表达=抽象表达\n【辨邻】 E E 段只点出 once a thing becomes popular, it will tend to become more popular\nstill，这是「累积」，不是「随机事件决定命运」；题干两个关键词 random events\nor preferences 与 fate 都不在 E 段，E 段不构成源头段。\nonce a thing becomes popular, it will tend to become more popular still", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "In his experiment, Cutting found that his subjects liked lesser known works better than canonical works because of more exposure.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Cutting 的实验：偏爱较不知名作品\n【定位】These were exposed four times as often. Afterwards, the students preferred them to the\ncanonical works, while a control group of students liked the canonical ones best. Cutting's\nstudents had grown to like those paintings more simply because they had seen them more.\n这些画的曝光次数是名作的四倍。结果学生更偏爱这些较不知名的画作，而对照组学生最喜\n欢名作。Cutting 的学生开始更喜欢这些画，仅仅因为他们看得更多。\n【改写】Cutting found that his subjects ↔ Cutting's students had grown to like those paintings\nsubjects=students 受试者上位词\nliked lesser known works better than canonical works ↔\nthe students preferred them to the canonical works\npreferred X to Y=liked X better than Y\nbecause of more exposure ↔ simply because they had seen them more\n曝光=看得更多\n【辨邻】 D D 段开篇 Cutting believes his experiment offers a clue as to how canons are\nformed 就显示这一段已经离开「实验本身」、走向「名作目录形成机制」；题干要求\n的是实验中学生的实际偏好这一发现，源头只能是 C 段。\nCutting believes his experiment offers a clue as to how canons are formed", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The author thinks the greatness of an art work still lies in its intrinsic value.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者立场：内在价值仍重要\n【定位】The intrinsic quality of a work of art is starting to seem like its least important attribute. But\nperhaps it's more significant than our social scientists allow. ... Secondly, some stuff is simply\nbetter than other stuff.\n艺术品的内在质量看似越来越不像是它最重要的属性。但或许它的分量比社会科学家们承认\n的要重得多。……其次，某些东西就是比另一些东西更出色。\n【改写】The author thinks ↔ But perhaps it's more significant than our social scientists allow\n无引述=作者直陈\nthe greatness of an art work still lies in its intrinsic value ↔\nsome stuff is simply better than other stuff\n口语断言=正式命题\nstill lies in its intrinsic value ↔ But perhaps it's more significant than our social scientists allow\nstill=反驳后保留\n【辨邻】 B B 段 some social scientists have been asking awkward questions of it 表达的是社\n会科学家对内在价值的怀疑，与题干要求的「作者认为内在价值仍重要」立场相反\n——主语和态度都不对。\nsome social scientists have been asking awkward questions of it, raising the possibility that artistic\ncanons(名作目录) are little more than fossilised historical accidents", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is true of critics as well as ordinary people that the popularity of artistic works is closely associated with publicity.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】批评家与大众都把名气和宣传挂钩\n【定位】After all, it's not just the masses who tend to rate what they see more often more highly. As\ncontemporary artists like Warhol and Damien Hirst have grasped, critics' praise is deeply\nentwined(交织) with publicity. \"Scholars\", Cutting argues, \"are no different from the public\nin the effects of mere exposure.\"\n毕竟，不仅仅是普罗大众才会把看得越多的东西评得越高。正如沃霍尔、达米恩·赫斯特这样\n的当代艺术家所悟到的，批评家的赞美与宣传深深交织在一起。Cutting 说：「就曝光效应\n而言，学者们与大众毫无二致。」\n【改写】It is true of critics as well as ordinary people ↔\nScholars, Cutting argues, are no different from the public in the effects of mere exposure\ncritics≈scholars，ordinary people≈the public\nthe popularity of artistic works is closely associated with publicity ↔\ncritics' praise is deeply entwined(交织) with publicity\n受欢迎程度=被赞美程度，挂钩对象都是 publicity\n【辨邻】 I I 段重点是 Nearly all the cultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in\ninherited opinion —— 偏好被前人继承，谈不到「批评家与大众都把流行与宣传挂\n钩」这一具体命题，关键锚点 critics 与 publicity 都缺位。\nNearly all the cultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in inherited opinion; our preferences are\nalways, to some extent, someone else's", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "We need to expose ourselves to more art and literature in order to tell the superior from the inferior.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】多接触作品才能分辨高下\n【定位】But that's why we need to see, and read, as much as we can. The more we're exposed to the\ngood and the bad, the better we are at telling the difference.\n但正因如此，我们才需要尽量多看、多读。我们越是接触好的与坏的，就越能分辨它们的\n高下。\n【改写】expose ourselves to more art and literature ↔ we need to see, and read, as much as we can\nsee/read=对艺术/文学的曝光\ntell the superior from the inferior ↔ the better we are at telling the difference\n辨别高下=分辨好坏的差别\n【辨邻】 C C 段 Cutting's students had grown to like those paintings more simply because\nthey had seen them more 强调曝光会带来偏爱、是一种带偏见的效应，与题干「通\n过多接触来分辨好坏」的处方意旨相反。\nCutting's students had grown to like those paintings more simply because they had seen them more", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "A study of the history of the greatest paintings suggests even a great work of art could experience years of neglect.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】伟大画作历史 + 长期被忽视\n【定位】When Watts looked into the history of \"the greatest painting of all time\", he discovered that,\nfor most of its life, the \"Mona Lisa\" remained in relative obscurity.\n当 Watts 去研究这幅「史上最伟大画作」的历史时，他发现这幅《蒙娜丽莎》一生中的大部\n分时间都相对默默无闻。\n【改写】A study of the history of the greatest paintings ↔\nWhen Watts looked into the history of \"the greatest painting of all time\"\nlook into=study 同义\ncould experience years of neglect ↔\nfor most of its life, the \"Mona Lisa\" remained in relative obscurity\n长期默默无闻=多年被忽视\n【辨邻】 G G 段重点是 Parisians were shocked at the theft of a painting to which, until\nthen, they had paid little attention——叙述盗窃事件后人们反应，没有承担「对其\n历史的研究」与「多年被忽视」这一全景式判断，只能作邻段排除。\nParisians were shocked at the theft of a painting to which, until then, they had paid little attention", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Culture is still used as a mark to distinguish one social class from another.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】文化作为阶层标识\n【定位】Although the rigid high-low distinction fell apart in the 1960s, we still use culture as a badge\nof identity. Today's fashion for eclecticism(折衷主义)—\"I love Bach, ABBA and Jay Z\"—is,\nShamus Khan, a Columbia University psychologist, argues, a new way for the middle class to\ndistinguish themselves from what they perceive to be the narrow tastes of those beneath them\nin the social hierarchy.\n虽然高雅与通俗的硬性区分在 1960 年代瓦解，我们依然把文化当作身份的徽章。哥伦比亚\n大学心理学家 Shamus Khan 认为，今天对折衷主义口味的追捧——「我喜欢巴赫，也喜欢\nABBA 和 Jay Z」——是中产阶级用来把自己与他们眼中口味狭隘的下层群体区分开来的新\n方式。\n【改写】Culture is still used as a mark ↔ we still use culture as a badge of identity\nbadge=mark 同义\nto distinguish one social class from another ↔\na new way for the middle class to distinguish themselves from what they perceive to be the\nnarrow tastes of those beneath them in the social hierarchy\n中产区分自己与下层=区分一个阶层与另一个阶层\n【辨邻】 I I 段 Nearly all the cultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in inherited\nopinion 重点在「意见被继承」，没有把 culture 跟 social class 挂钩，关键锚点\nsocial class 缺位，不能视为源头段。\nNearly all the cultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in inherited opinion; our preferences are\nalways, to some extent, someone else's", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Opinions about and preferences for cultural objects are often inheritable.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对文化对象的偏好可继承\n【定位】Nearly all the cultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in inherited opinion; our\npreferences are always, to some extent, someone else's.\n我们消费的几乎所有文化对象，都包裹着继承来的意见；我们的偏好在某种程度上始终是别\n人的偏好。\n【改写】Opinions about and preferences for cultural objects ↔\ncultural objects we consume arrive wrapped in inherited opinion; our preferences\n意见+偏好+文化对象三词对位\nare often inheritable ↔\narrive wrapped in inherited opinion; our preferences are always, to some extent, someone\nelse's\n继承属性的同义改写\n【辨邻】 H H 段 Paintings, poems and pop songs are buoyed(使浮起) or sunk by random\nevents or preferences that turn into waves of influence, passing down the\ngenerations 谈的是「影响波代代相传」，但没有点出「意见与偏好本身被继承」，关\n键词 inherited/inheritable 不在 H 段。\nPaintings, poems and pop songs are buoyed(使浮起) or sunk by random events or preferences that\nturn into waves of influence, passing down the generations", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do many people think is the biggest problem facing Janet Yellen?", "options": ["A) Lack of money.", "B) Subprime crisis.", "C) Unemployment.", "D) Social instability."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do many people think is the biggest problem facing Janet Yellen? 是 What 引\n出的事实问句，答案就在原文，无需推理。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What do many people think，其中 is the biggest problem facing Janet\nYellen 是 think 的宾语从句；facing Janet Yellen 是分词短语作 problem 的后置定语，相\n当于 that faces Janet Yellen。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】If you believe, as many do, that unemployment is the major economic and social concern\nof our day, then it is no stretch to think Yellen is the most powerful person in the world\nright now.\n如果你像许多人一样相信失业是当今最重大的经济与社会问题，那么认为 Yellen 是当今世\n界最有权力的人就一点也不夸张。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nLack of money. 在原文中找不到任何对应——P2 谈的是 Fed 向市场\"砸钱\" $3.8\ntrillion money dump，恰恰相反。\nB) 张冠李戴\nSubprime crisis. 来自 warnings about the subprime(次级债) meltdown——那是\nYellen 危机早期的预警内容，不是 many people think 当前 Yellen 面临的最大问题。\nC) ✓ 正确\nunemployment is the major economic and social concern of our day ↔ Unemployment.\nthe major economic and social concern ↔ biggest problem\n预判答案是\"失业\"，Unemployment. 与之完全一致，无任何争议。\nD) 过度推断\nSocial instability. 是从 economic and social concern 中 social 一词过度延伸出来的\n——\"社会层面的关切\"不等于\"社会不稳定\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did Yellen help the Fed do to tackle the 2008 financial crisis?", "options": ["A) Take effective measures to curb inflation.", "B) Deflate the bubbles in the American economy.", "C) Formulate policies to help financial institutions.", "D) Pour money into the market through asset buying."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did Yellen help the Fed do to tackle the 2008 financial crisis? 直问一个已发生的\n具体动作，无推理信号。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What did Yellen help the Fed do，help 后省略 to 接动词原形 do；to tackle\nthe 2008 financial crisis 是不定式短语作目的状语。无陷阱。\n【定位】Throughout the 2008 financial crisis and the recession and recovery that followed, central\nbanks have taken on the role of stimulators of last resort, holding up the global economy with\nvast amounts of money in the form of asset buying. Yellen, previously a Fed vice chair, was\none of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump.\n在整个 2008 年金融危机以及随之而来的衰退与复苏期间，各国央行充当起\"最后的刺激\n者\"，通过大规模购买资产向全球经济注入巨额资金。Yellen 此前是 Fed 副主席，是 Fed 那\n场 3.8 万亿美元\"撒钱\"计划的主要设计者之一。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTake effective measures to curb inflation. 与原文方向相反——Fed 是大规模向市\n场注资（应对通缩与衰退），不是\"遏制通胀\"。通胀只在 P4 作为\"少数专家的担忧\"被\n提及。\nB) 张冠李戴\nDeflate the bubbles in the American economy. 来自 deflate bubbles——那是\"未\n来退出刺激\"时要做的事，不是 to tackle the 2008 financial crisis 时 Yellen 帮 Fed 做\n的事。\nC) 无中生有\nFormulate policies to help financial institutions. 在原文中找不到对应——Fed 是\n向市场\"砸钱\"，不是\"为金融机构定政策\"；且 P6 显示 Yellen 反而要加强对金融业的\n监管。\nD) ✓ 正确\nvast amounts of money in the form of asset buying ↔\nPour money into the market through asset buying.\nYellen...was one of the principal architects of the Fed's $3.8 trillion money dump ↔\nPour money into the market through asset buying.\n预判\"通过买资产向市场注资\"与 Pour money into the market through asset buying.\n完全一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is a greater concern of the general public?", "options": ["A) Recession.", "B) Deflation.", "C) Inequality.", "D) Income."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is a greater concern of the general public? 直问\"什么是 X\"，是事实型问句，答案\n在原文。\n【拆句】题干 What is a greater concern of the general public?：主干是 What is a concern，\ngreater 是 concern 的前置定语（比较级形容词），of the general public 是介词短语作\nconcern 的后置定语。比较级是关键信号，提示原文存在对照。\n【定位】But with wages still relatively flat and the economy increasingly divided between the well-off\nand the long-term unemployed, more people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩)\nthat would aggravate the economy's problems.\n但由于工资仍相对停滞，且经济日益在富裕阶层与长期失业者之间分化，更多人担忧的是相\n反方向的问题——通货紧缩，它将使经济困境进一步恶化。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nRecession. 来自 the recession and recovery that followed——那是 2008 危机之后\n的\"过去衰退\"，不是当前\"更多人担心\"的对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmore people worry about the opposite, deflation(通货紧缩) ↔ Deflation.\nmore people ↔ general public\n预判答案是 deflation（通缩），Deflation. 与之完全一致。\nC) 过度推断\nInequality. 来自 the economy increasingly divided between the well-off and the\nlong-term unemployed——那只是\"通缩\"担忧的背景描述，不是\"担忧本身\"。把背景\n升级为答案，属于过度推断。\nD) 无中生有\nIncome. 在原文中没有作为\"普通大众的担忧对象\"出现——wages still relatively flat 只\n是引出通缩担忧的论据之一，工资 ≠ 担忧的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is Yellen likely to do in her position as the Fed chief?", "options": ["A) Develop a new monetary program.", "B) Restore public confidence.", "C) Tighten financial regulation.", "D) Reform the credit system."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is Yellen likely to do in her position as the Fed chief? 直问一个具体行动，无推理\n信号词。\n【拆句】题干 What is Yellen likely to do in her position as the Fed chief?：主干 What is Yellen\nlikely to do，be likely to do 是固定结构表\"很可能做某事\"；in her position as the Fed\nchief 是介词短语作状语，as 引出 position 的同位语。\n【定位】Unlike many past Fed leaders, Yellen is not one to buy into the finance industry's argument\nthat it should be left alone to regulate itself. She knows all along the Fed has been too slack\non regulation of finance.\n与许多前任 Fed 主席不同，Yellen 并不认同金融业\"应被放任自我监管\"的主张。她一直知道\nFed 在金融监管上太过松懈。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nDevelop a new monetary program. 在原文中没有对应——文中提到\n的\"unconventional monetary program\"是 P2 已经在做的 3.8 万亿计划，而 get the\nFed and the markets out of 表明她要做的是退出该计划，不是\"开发新计划\"。\nB) 无中生有\nRestore public confidence. 在原文中找不到对应——文中既未提到\"public\nconfidence\"这一概念，也未把\"恢复信心\"列为 Yellen 的任务。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe Fed has been too slack on regulation of finance ↔ Tighten financial regulation.\nYellen is likely to address the issue ↔ Tighten financial regulation.\n预判\"收紧金融监管\"与 Tighten financial regulation. 完全一致。\nD) 过度推断\nReform the credit system. 来自 it creates another credit crisis 的 credit 一词\n——\"信贷危机\"只是被警告要避免的事，文章从未提 Yellen 要\"改革信贷体系\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How does Alan Blinder portray Yellen?", "options": ["A) She possesses strong persuasive power.", "B) She has confidence in what she is doing.", "C) She is one of the world's greatest economists.", "D) She is the most powerful Fed chief in history."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How does Alan Blinder portray Yellen? 问 X 如何描绘 Y，本质是问 X 说了什么、说了哪\n个特点。\n【拆句】题干 How does Alan Blinder portray Yellen?：主干是 How does Alan Blinder portray\nYellen，portray 后直接接宾语 Yellen，How 问描绘方式。结构简单。\n【定位】As Princeton Professor Alan Blinder says, \"She's smart as a whip, deeply logical, willing to\nargue but also a good listener. She can persuade without creating hostility.\"\n正如普林斯顿大学教授 Alan Blinder 所言：\"她绝顶聪明，逻辑性极强，愿意争论但也是个\n好听众。她能说服别人，而又不挑起敌意。\"\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nShe can persuade without creating hostility ↔ She possesses strong persuasive power.\nwithout creating hostility ↔ strong persuasive power\n引语前半段并列形容词（smart / logical / willing to argue / good listener）都是铺\n垫，最后一句 She can persuade without creating hostility 才是这段评价的整合性落\n点；选项 A 直接对应该落点。\nB) 无中生有\nShe has confidence in what she is doing. 在 Alan Blinder 的引语中找不到对应——\n引语谈的是聪明、逻辑、能说服，没有提及\"对自己所做之事有信心\"。\nC) 张冠李戴\nShe is one of the world's greatest economists. 借了 A star economist known for\nher groundbreaking work on labor markets 的描述——但那是文章作者的评价，不\n是 Alan Blinder 引语里的话。题问 Alan Blinder 怎么描绘，答案必须来自引语内。\nD) 过度推断\nShe is the most powerful Fed chief in history. 借了 Yellen is the most powerful\nperson in the world right now 的最高级——但原文说的是\"当今世界最有权力的人\"，\n且是作者本人的假设性表述，不是 Alan Blinder 说的，也不是\"史上最有权力的 Fed\n主席\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author assume might be the primary reason that people would struggle with each other?", "options": ["A) To get their share of clean air.", "B) To pursue a comfortable life.", "C) To gain a higher social status.", "D) To seek economic benefits."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author assume might be the primary reason that people would\nstruggle with each other? 问作者所设想的具体原因，要在原文找到对应句即可，不需跨\n段综合。\n【拆句】题干主干为 What ... might be the primary reason ...，其后 that 引导同位语从句修饰\nreason，说明这个 reason 的内容是 people would struggle with each other。理解时注\n意 assume 弱化口气，但并不改变事实问法，仍按细节题处理。\n【定位】Perhaps struggle unfolds first, not at an economic or social level, but over the appropriation\nof air, essential to life itself.\n也许人类的相争最先发生的层面，并非经济或社会，而是围绕对空气——这一生命本身赖以\n为生的东西——的争夺。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe appropriation of air ↔ get their share of clean air\nessential to life itself ↔ clean air\n预判\"为争夺空气\"在四个选项中只有 To get their share of clean air. 字面对应；其余三\n项均与 not at an economic or social level 被作者明确排除的层面挂钩。\nB) 无中生有\n选项把动机定位在 pursue a comfortable life，但原文从未提及 comfort/comfortable\nlife，全段说的是 essential to life itself 的基本生存条件。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明确说 not at an economic or social level，选项却落在 a higher social status\n这一社会层面，与原文 not ... social level 的否定形成直接反面。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项落在 economic benefits 这一经济层面，但原文 not at an economic or social\nlevel 已明确否定经济层面是 primary reason。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author accuse western politicians of?", "options": ["A) Depriving common people of the right to clean air.", "B) Giving priority to theory rather than practical action.", "C) Offering preferential treatment to wealthy countries.", "D) Failing to pass laws to curb environmental pollution."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author accuse western politicians of? 是对原文某条具体指控的转述，仍\n属于客观信息检索，归为细节题。\n【拆句】题干结构是 accuse sb. of doing/sth.，宾语从 of 后引出。理解时注意 accuse 隐含 \"作者\n认为对方做错了\"，所以原文定位句必然带批评语气。\n【定位】As for politicians, despite proposing curbs on environmental pollution, they have not yet\ncalled for it to be made a crime. Wealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay\nfor it.\n至于政客们，尽管提出了遏制环境污染的措施，却尚未呼吁把污染入罪。富裕国家甚至被允\n许只要肯付钱就可以污染。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只批评富国花钱排污的安排，并未声称政客\"剥夺普通人呼吸权\"；选项 Depriving\ncommon people of the right to clean air 是从 \"富国可排污\" 推一步到 \"普通人无\n权\"，跨越文本。\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文 neither materialist nor idealist theoreticians give enough consideration 批评\n的是\"理论家\"轻视该课题，而非政客\"理论先于行动\"；选项 Giving priority to theory\nrather than practical action 把 theoreticians 的问题嫁接到 politicians 头上。\nC) ✓ 正确\nWealthy countries are even allowed to pollute if they pay for it ↔\nOffering preferential treatment to wealthy countries\nWealthy countries ↔ wealthy countries\n预判\"纵容富国花钱排污\"在四个选项中只有 C 提到 wealthy countries 并以\npreferential treatment 概括 even allowed 的特殊待遇；其余三项均偏离\"富国—付费\n—允许污染\"的指控核心。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 they have not yet called for it to be made a crime 说的是\"尚未呼吁把污染入\n罪\"，而非 Failing to pass laws to curb environmental pollution 的\"未通过法律遏制\n污染\"；前者只批评\"未升级到刑事立法\"，后者直接否定了 proposing curbs 这一已发生\n的行动。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author try to draw our closest attention to?", "options": ["A) The massive burning of fossil fuels.", "B) Our relationship to the plant world.", "C) The capacity of plants to renew polluted air.", "D) Large-scale deforestation across the world."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author try to draw our closest attention to? 问作者点出的具体关注对\n象，原文应有明确陈述，无需推断。\n【拆句】题干为 draw sb.'s attention to sth. 的结构，闭合介词 to 引出关注对象，回答时只需把 to\n后的内容补齐。\n【定位】The interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention is that which exists between\nourselves and the plant world.\n我们必须给予最密切关注的相互依存关系，正是我们自己与植物世界之间的那种关系。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n选项把关注焦点缩到 The massive burning of fossil fuels 这一污染源头；但原文\nrapid deforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels is an\nexplosive recipe 只是举例说明灾难成因之一，并非作者要我们\"最密切关注\"的对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\nbetween ourselves and the plant world ↔ Our relationship to the plant world\nThe interdependence to which we must pay the closest attention ↔ relationship\n预判\"人与植物的相互依存\"在四个选项中只有 Our relationship to the plant world. 同\n时含 ourselves（our）+ plant world 两个要素；其余三项要么只取植物的一个功能，\n要么只取人类的一种行为，均偏离 interdependence 的双向关系内核。\nC) 以偏概全\n选项把关注焦点缩到 The capacity of plants to renew polluted air；但原文 their\ncapacity to renew the air polluted by industry has long reached its limit 提到该能\n力时，重点是\"已达极限\"，是论证 interdependence 的依据而非作者要我们\"最密切关\n注\"的对象本身。\nD) 以偏概全\n选项把关注焦点缩到 Large-scale deforestation across the world，原文 rapid\ndeforestation combined with the massive burning of fossil fuels 同样只是与 A 并\n列出现的灾难成因之一，并非 closest attention 所指向的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How can human beings accomplish the goal of protecting the plant according to the author?", "options": ["A) By showing respect for plants.", "B) By preserving all forms of life.", "C) By tapping all natural resources.", "D) By pooling their efforts together."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How can human beings accomplish the goal of protecting the plant according to\nthe author? 仅问完成路径，原文给出具体条件即可作答。\n【拆句】题干主干 How can human beings accomplish the goal ...，of protecting the plant 是\n介词短语作 goal 的后置定语；according to the author 表明答案必须来自原文。\n【定位】This task is simultaneously ethical and political because it can be discharged only when each\ntakes it upon herself or himself and only when it is accomplished together with others.\n这一任务同时是伦理的与政治的，因为它只有在每个人自身承担起责任，并且只有在与他人\n一同完成时，才得以履行。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项 By showing respect for plants 在原文找不到对应：作者强调的是\"分享生\n命\"和\"共同承担\"，并未出现 respect/respectful 等\"尊重\"语义。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 By preserving all forms of life 把原文 sharing life 的\"分享生命\"偷换成\npreserving all forms of life 的\"保存一切生命形式\"；preserve（保存/保护）与 share\n（共享）在动作目标上不同——前者强调 \"留住\"，后者强调 \"共有\"。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 By tapping all natural resources 主张\"开发利用一切自然资源\"，与原文 dividing\nlife into so-called natural or human resources diminishes it 直接对立——作者明确\n反对把生命当作\"自然资源\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntogether with others ↔ pooling their efforts together\nonly when it is accomplished ↔ By\n预判\"共同努力\"在四个选项中只有 By pooling their efforts together. 同时含 together\n与 efforts 两个要素；其他三项要么把方式偷换成 \"尊重植物/保护一切生命\"，要么走向\n反面 \"开发自然资源\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest we do in order not just to survive?", "options": ["A) Expand the sphere of living.", "B) Develop nature's potentials.", "C) Share life with nature.", "D) Allocate the resources."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest we do in order not just to survive? 问作者明确给出的\n建议，且限定在结尾段的对照结构里，按细节题做。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author suggest we do ...；in order not just to survive 是目的\n状语，含否定副词 not just，理解为 \"不只是为了活着\"，暗示与生存对立的更高目标。\n【定位】Perhaps then we would finally begin to live, rather than being concerned with bare survival.\n也许那时，我们才能真正开始\"生活\"，而不只是为勉强生存而忧虑。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n选项 Expand the sphere of living 摘取 sharing life expands and enhances the\nsphere of the living 中的 expand 一词当主张；但原文是说\"分享生命带来扩张\"，扩张\n是结果而非作者建议本身。\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 Develop nature's potentials 主张\"开发自然的潜能\"，与原文 rather than a web\nof quantifiable objects or productive potentialities at our disposal 直接对立——作\n者明确反对把自然视为\"可供调用的生产潜能\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhumans learn to share life, both with each other and with plants ↔ Share life with nature\nshare life ↔ Share life\n预判\"把自己与空气植物视作生命的共同维护者\"在四个选项中只有 Share life with\nnature. 同时保留 share life 与 nature（覆盖 plants/air）两个要素；其他三项要么截取\n一句话的某个动词（expand sphere）、要么落到被作者明确反对的\"开发资源\"上。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 Allocate the resources 主张\"分配资源\"，但原文 dividing life into so-called\nnatural or human resources diminishes it 明确批评\"把生命分成自然/人类资源\"——\n分配资源正是被否定的做法。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
